{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/DECISION-PAGE-2026-11-16.md docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/week/WEEK-RESULT.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/SLICE-RESULT.md clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/AUDIT-RESULT.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/unlisted-parts/fresh-a2/line5/RESULT.md docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/unlisted-parts/fresh-a2/postponement/AUDIT-RESULT.md docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/unlisted-parts/fresh-a2/fiscal-note/RESULT.md'","aggregated_output":"# Why the fresh topics miss line 5 (D604's trace), 2026-11-16, $0\n\n`line5_trace.py` → `line5-trace-output.txt`. It reads the stored fresh reading (`poc/data/clean-fresh-a2-2026-11-16/choices/reading`)\nwith the gate-1 scorer's own functions, for the control arm (the frozen step). It reproduces the recorded line 5: 2 of 5 right with\nunresolved points counted for the answer, 1 of 5 with them counted against.\n\n## Topic by topic\n\n| Topic (kind) | What blocks it, counted for / against | How settled | Reaches what a reader of the product sees? | Existing record |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| clean-14 (theme figure) | XR / XR | Passes 1 and 3: both third readers fail XR (the reference: IDC's \"19% growth\" forecast for a unit count). Pass 2 split. | **Not in these cases** (corrected in round 146, see below). | Event reference has no bounded repair (D557, D558); \"what counts as an expectation\" is an open form issue. |\n| clean-15 (theme figure) | L, XR / L, XR | L: pass 1 both first readers; pass 2 both third readers; pass 3 split. XR: pass 1 both first readers. | **Yes, for L:** a part the question leaves out, now shown in the card's list of parts not judged. XR: the same visible choice either way (below). | Line 2's class; no sourced main-part rule (D580); tuning stopped (D586, D391). |\n| clean-16 (AI bill) | none / none: three justified refusals | Both families: N holds. | Right. | |\n| clean-17 (event) | R, XR / R, XR | R: pass 3 both third readers (the wait-as-ruling-out class); pass 1 split. XR: all three passes, both first readers. | **Yes, for R:** the Ruled-out rung's words. XR: no (the judge never sees an event's reference, D557). | The postponement boundary, on Alex's page (D591). |\n| clean-18 (company figure) | none / D | D split on all three passes (Codex fails, Claude holds). | **Yes,** the due day is shown, and it is code's D414 day, 60 days after year-end. Readers who fail it read the request's fiscal-year note (\"around 2027-02; use that day\"), which contradicts code. | The reader discrepancy (D590): read alone, both families hold D on the class. The request-and-code contradiction (`universe.py:136` against D414) is recorded and not changed. |\n\n## What this gives\n\n- **On fresh topics, line 5's misses reduce to four recorded things:**\n  - reference choices (XR, on 3 of 4 written topics, and the only blocker of clean-14). In these cases a rule-following answer would\n    produce the same answer choices; see the section below;\n  - a stopped class (L);\n  - an unresolved policy boundary (R, Alex's);\n  - a reader discrepancy on Alex's own rule (D).\n- **No common, sourced product mechanism is found.** By D604's rule this closes the route to a candidate. The gate stays unmet; a\n  later candidate is not excluded.\n- **A diagnostic recount of the stored marks, not a gate result and not a change of any line.** Without XR, line 5 on the fresh\n  topics would read 3 of 5 (unresolved for) or 2 of 5 (against). The development map's 9 or 7 of 13 (`gate1-stick/RESULT.md`) was\n  counted without both L and XR, not without XR alone.\n- **Limits:** one week, one draw, 5 topics, control arm only.\n\n## Corrected in round 146, and the source-backed reading (D604's unfinished part)\n\n**My error.** I wrote that no product reader sees the ladder's reference. That is wrong for figures. `ladder_step.write_view` puts the\nreference into the middle answer choice's words, which the judge is shown and the page carries:\n- with a stated number, \"(the reference when the question opened: \"…\")\";\n- with a last reported amount, \"(the amount last reported …)\";\n- otherwise \"(no reference figure when the question opened)\".\n\nAn event's reference is used by no rung. I had relied on an older finding and a search for the word in `bundle.py`, without following\nthe writer.\n\n**Each XR failure, read beside its article.** The articles are in `cases/cases.json`; the readers' reasons are in\n`choices/reading/third-reader` and `readers-answers`.\n\n| Topic, passes | What the article states | The answer's reference | Readers | What the judge and page see |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| clean-14, all three | \"China's broader AI accelerator market reached about 2.008 million units in the first half … IDC expects total accelerator shipments to grow 19% in 2026\". No full-year 2025 count, so no expected count is stated. | null | Claude: the 19% is an expectation for this very quantity, so it should be quoted (kind `expected`, no number). Codex: a growth rate, not a count, so null holds in packet 1 and fails in packet 2. | **The same either way.** With no number in the figure's unit, a quoted words-only reference gives the same middle choice as null: \"(no reference figure when the question opened)\". |\n| clean-15, pass 1 | Microsoft: \"Starting next calendar year, we will scale our Maia family to multi-gigawatt deployments\". | null | Both first readers fail, so it was settled without a third reader. On passes 2 and 3, and on the A2 arm, both families' third readers rule that a null reference is right, because it is one company's plan, not an expectation for the combined capacity. | The same either way, since no number. The fail is a reading inconsistency across passes. |\n| clean-17, all three (event) | \"Taiwan will tighten export controls on high-end AI processors and servers, mirroring U.S. rules\". | null | Both first readers fail: an expected event belongs in the reference. | **Nothing:** an event's reference is used by no rung. |\n\n**What it gives:**\n\n- **Every XR failure on these topics leaves what the judge and the page are shown unchanged.** An answer that followed the rule\n  exactly would produce the same answer choices: a words-only reference for clean-14 and clean-17, or null for clean-15.\n  - The rule allows \"no number the article does not state\".\n  - None of these articles states a number in the figure's unit.\n  - An event's reference never reaches a rung.\n- **The disputes are about the rule's reach:** whether a growth rate counts as an expectation for a unit count, and whether one\n  company's plan does for a combined figure. \"What counts as an expectation\" is the open form issue already recorded.\n- **The narrower claim (round 147):** this checks the writer's behaviour on the stored answers; it is not a test that a new model\n  request would leave every other field unchanged.\n- **Under D604 and round 146's criterion this closes the route.** It found disputed reference choices with no product consequence and\n  no sourced general mechanism. The gate stays unmet; no paid work follows without a new candidate.\n- **Observation, outside the line marks:**\n  - A Claude first reader's G remark on clean-14: the middle range (3.5 to 4.0 million) sits below the first half's annual rate\n    (about 4.0 million) and below what IDC's growth implies.\n  - G is not one of the line marks.\n  - The ranges are product-visible, but one reader of one family on one topic is a lead, not a class.\n# The postponed-event class, audited (D606's step), 2026-11-16, $0\n\n`audit_collect.py` → `audit-answers.jsonl`: every event answer the frozen answer-choice step wrote in the stored gate-1 readings.\n- Probe readings contribute their control sends only, by their sealed keys; changed arms, plants, and runs 2 and 3 before the frozen\n  step are left out.\n- That gives 246 answers shown, 195 distinct, over 43 topics.\n- The census's \"296\" counted more readings' answers; this audit uses only the frozen step's own, which is the narrower base.\n\n`audit-screen.txt` and `audit-class.txt` hold the timing ruling-outs, picked by a narrow screen: \"will not … by/during\", postpone,\ndefer, delay, \"moved … to after\". A sample of 12 unpicked decisions found one missed form (\"moved … to after\"), now added. The\nresult is 34 distinct answers (45 shown) over 7 matters, each read whole, with its rungs and every first and third reader's R and\nreason.\n\n## The matters\n\nCounts are from one grouping, by the question's words (`audit-counts.txt`). An earlier table here was from a coarser grouping and\ndid not add up (council round 149).\n\n| Matter (decider) | Distinct answers | Shown | First readers' R on the answers shown | Readers' reasons for a fail |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Vera Rubin first shipments by 2026-12-31 (NVIDIA) | 10 | 15 | 12 both hold; 3 split (third readers: 2 both hold, 1 split) | \"a delay rather than a decision against first shipments as a whole\" |\n| Vera Rubin volume shipments and revenue in FY2027 (NVIDIA) | 9 | 11 | 9 both hold, 1 split, 1 both fail | \"can be a forecast of delay rather than a decision against the event\", plus a W about the 10-K's day |\n| Kyber NVL144 / Rubin Ultra first shipments (NVIDIA) | 8 | 11 | 9 both hold, 2 split | **another class:** the question asks \"during 2027\" but Yes says \"by 2027-12-31\" |\n| Rubin volume ramp by fiscal Q4 FY2027 (NVIDIA) | 3 | 3 | all both hold | |\n| Groq 3 LPX volume shipments in fiscal Q3 FY2027 (NVIDIA) | 2 | 2 | all both hold | |\n| Hugging Face acquisition closing by 2028-03-24 (NVIDIA or Hugging Face) | 1 | 2 | both fail, twice (settled, no third reader) | \"will not close by the date … a forecast of delay … the deal still pending\" |\n| Taiwan's export controls by 2027-01-31 (an authority) | 1 | 1 | split; both third readers fail | the wait (D420) |\n| **Total** | **34** (company: 33 over 6 matters) | **45** (company: 44) | | |\n\n## The whole rung, read as the judge and the page see it\n\nIn every answer, rung 1 states the event **with its date**. Examples:\n- \"NVIDIA begins shipping Vera Rubin by December 31, 2026 …\";\n- \"NVIDIA reports Vera Rubin volume customer shipments with first revenue recognition on or before the end of FY2027\";\n- \"NVIDIA completes the Hugging Face acquisition by March 24, 2028\".\n\nThe Ruled-out rung is the model's decision followed by code's sentence: \"This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the\nevent of rung 1 as a whole, … was made after <article day> and stands on <due day>, and only when rung 1 does not.\"\n\n- **A company's formal announcement that the event will not happen by the due day** is a decision against rung 1's event as written:\n  the dated event, as a whole. The reader brief's R row says the same: \"the `Yes` condition is the event the question asks about; the\n  first sentence of the `Ruled out` condition … is a decision against that event as a whole … and not the event's merely being still\n  awaited\".\n  - **Qualified in round 149:** an announcement is not necessarily a decision. \"It will not happen by the date\" can be a forecast of\n    delay rather than the company deciding against the dated event. Some dissenting reasons draw exactly that line (\"can be a\n    forecast of delay\"), so it is too strong to say they all ignored the date. Any future reading keeps a forecast-versus-decision\n    guard. The frozen step's sentences mostly name a decision (\"formally announces\", \"formally decides to defer\", \"postponed\"); some\n    say only \"announces that … will not …\", which is where that guard bites.\n- **A delay inside the deadline** cannot rule out: code's \"only when rung 1 does not\" and \"stands on the due day\" leave rung 1 to\n  hold if the event happens by the due day.\n- **A changed decision** that no longer stands on the due day does not rule out, by code's sentence.\n- **A cancellation** rules out in every answer read.\n- **An authority's proceeding** follows D420 and the brief's own clause: its formal abandonment or withdrawal rules it out, and its\n  going on is the wait.\n  - Taiwan p3's \"will not adopt … by January 31, 2027\" names a timetable while the proceeding goes on. That is the wait, so it is a\n    misapplication.\n  - It is one send of three; the topic's other two name abandonment or withdrawal.\n  - The model's own account is on record (`taiwan-account-output.txt`): it carried the deadline into the decision.\n\n## What it gives\n\n- **No general model error is shown in the company-delay class.** Across 6 company matters (33 distinct answers), the frozen step's\n  ruling outs read as decisions agree with rung 1's dated event, code's sentence, D409, D416 and the reader brief. The open edge is\n  forecast against decision, not a missing rule.\n- **D606's provisional reading misstates these questions.** That reading was \"a formal postponement where the event remains possible\n  reads Still open\". Read against the undated event, it would leave a dated question \"Still open\" after its date while the company\n  has already announced it will not happen by then. **By D606's own reversal condition** (\"if worked cases show it misstates the\n  question an analyst holds\"), the reading is reversed:\n  - for an event with a due day, the event is rung 1's, with its date;\n  - a decision that it will not happen by the due day, standing on the due day, rules it out;\n  - an authority's proceeding follows D420.\n- **The authority kind is one isolated misapplication** (Taiwan p3, one send of three).\n- **By D606's criterion the route closes,** since it found only isolated misapplications and no general rule is missing. Line 3 stays\n  unmet on the fresh week by that one send.\n- **Found on the way, another class, not pursued here:** two Kyber answers whose Yes (\"by 2027-12-31\") is wider than their question\n  (\"during 2027\"). That is a question-to-rung window mismatch (the Q and R marks), 2 answers on 1 matter.\n- **My error:** in round 148 I proposed \"Still open\" as my lean. My recorded lean on Alex's page (D591) was the opposite, \"rules out\",\n  and I did not say I had changed it. The audit supports the D591 lean.\n# The shared fiscal-year note, audited (D607's step), 2026-11-16, $0\n\n**Who reads the note** (`universe.fiscal_note`):\n\n- production extraction's request (`src/hivemind/extraction.py:387`, frozen);\n- the answer-choice step's payload (`clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/stage_a/stage_a.py:144`, frozen as the ladder step, D426);\n- the gate-1 readers' packets (`stage_a/build_reader_packets.py:35`).\n\nFor NVDA the note ends: \"FY2027's full-year figures are published in the annual report around 2027-02; use that day as the due\ndate\". The shared function is not edited.\n\n**What the clause could reach in the answer-choice step:**\n\n- **The shown day (`ladder_step.due_day_of`):** code replaces the model's day only when the model chose the basis\n  `expected_publication` (D414: year-end plus 60 days for a company figure). A `stated` day is kept. So the clause could reach the\n  product only through the model's choice of basis, or through the `stated` days it writes.\n- **`basis_census.py`** → `basis-census-output.txt`: 202 distinct company-question answers from the frozen step (probe readings'\n  control sends only):\n\n  | Basis | The day falls in the note's month | Another day |\n  |---|---|---|\n  | `expected_publication` (code sets D414's day) | 23 | 102 |\n  | `stated` (code keeps the answer's day) | 3 | 74 |\n\n  - The 23 `expected_publication` answers in the note's month are model days that code replaces.\n  - The 74 `stated` answers on another day are, as read, events and amounts with a deadline the question itself names: shipments by\n    2026-12-31, the Hugging Face deal by 2028-03-24, a guarantee by 2027-12-31.\n  - The 3 `stated` answers in the note's month are one matter: \"Rubin volume ramp by January 31, 2027, shown by the fiscal Q4\n    earnings release\", dated 2027-02-24 or 2027-02-28, the release that settles it. That is defensible as stated, and not shown to\n    come from the note.\n- **Year:** the year mapping (\"this year is FY2027 …\") is in the note's other clauses, which no change proposed here would touch.\n- **Refusal and rung:** nothing in the stored answers links either to the due-day clause.\n- **The check before code's replacement (round 150):** for `expected_publication`, code first refuses a written day before the\n  year's end (`ladder_step.py` check 5). The note's \"around 2027-02\" gives the model a day after the year-end. Removing the clause\n  could therefore raise code refusals, so the clause is not inert. A change would need that measured.\n- **The D split** (D590: Codex readers fail D on code-set days): the readers' packet shows the same note beside D414's day.\n  - Removing the clause from the step's request would not change the packet.\n  - Changing the packet is a change of the reading form, which D590 decided against.\n  - **The cause of the split is not established:** the D-only diagnostic did not reproduce the split, so the packet's note is a\n    candidate cause, not a shown one.\n\n**Result by D607's criterion:**\n\n- No product-visible effect is shown: the shown day is D414's or the question's own deadline.\n- The reader split's cause is not established, so no gate-relevant effect of the request is shown.\n- **Closed as a cleanup-only lead.** The step's request carries a due-day instruction that code overrides (AGENTS §3 and §6). No new\n  version and no paid send follow.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/SLICE-RESULT.md clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/AUDIT-RESULT.md'","aggregated_output":"# The slice (D450): one fresh day through the wired path, live — reading (2026-11-13)\n\n**Answer: the wiring works live.** Both draws completed (`poc/data/clean-e2e-slice-2026-11-13/`: clean `s1`, production `sp1`, both\n2026-07-01, launched 05:31:38, ended 05:39:40); every component ran with real sends on one shared ledger; no stop, no overrun, no refusal,\nno retry; 60 ledger lines, every one attributed to its component, summing to the ledger's spent exactly. A wiring check, not a draw and not\nevidence of quality (WIRING-DESIGN.md §G).\n\n## Spend and time\n\n| component | time | attempts | ledger charge | archive tokens at `model.PRICES` | note |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Step 1 (c8, 13 items, sequential) | 7 min 23 s | 13 | $0.1324 | $0.1324 | equal |\n| Step 2 (embeddings 1, Jev 22) | 3 s | 23 | $0.0110 | Jev $0.0036 | Jev charged at three times its tokens, embeddings likewise (5,874 tokens) |\n| cases | 1 s | 0 | 0 | — | 2 cases, equal to the theses the trace opened |\n| ladder step (pass 1) | 16 s | 2 | $0.0021 | $0.0024 at the full input rate | the ledger counts cached input at its share |\n| judge | 18 s | 2 | $0.0019 | $0.0021 at the full input rate | same |\n| production v118 (16 workers) | 41 s | 20 (LLM 15, Jev 5) | $0.0119 | $0.0150 at the full rate, Jev once | same |\n| **total** | | 60 | **$0.1593** | about $0.152 at `model.call`'s formula | |\n\nSpend under D439: $7.11 before, about $0.15 now (ledger $0.16): **about $7.26 of $40**.\n\n**Projection for the full run** (an estimate from this day's per-item rates and the stored runs, labelled as such): Step 1 about $0.010\nan item → about $2.5 a clean draw (243 items), sequential about 2 h 20 min a draw; Step 2 about $0.36 of ledger a draw (the stored u1);\nthe ladder step three passes about $0.04 a draw; the judge about $0.14 a draw (Stage B); production about $0.45 a draw (q1–q3). Three of\neach: about **$10.4 of ledger**, inside $15.00 − $0.16.\n\n## What each component produced (read by Claude, every answer)\n\n- **Step 1** (13 answers beside their articles): two theses opened, the same two matters the stored draw u1 opened that day: the amount of\n  credit support NVIDIA will have extended to AI cloud partners at its next annual report (from NVIDIA's own announcement), and whether\n  NVIDIA will guarantee OpenAI's Ohio data-centre lease by 2027-12-31 (from The Information's report carried by moomoo). Seven evidence\n  items link to them (the announcement, Sharon AI and Firmus as first partners, the CoreWeave $6.3bn backstop, the later $3.5bn lease\n  guarantees, the equity stakes, the CFO's blog post). The other articles (Intel's $5bn stake and Xeon 6 choice, Valar's nuclear data\n  centre, Wistron in Texas, NN's liquid cooling, Palantir and Nemotron, Verkada, an RTX 5090 price piece, an agentic-RL blog post, Burry's\n  bets) opened nothing and their statements are unlinked, as the standard's \"lean cautious\" asks. One field to log: the Ohio item's\n  original party is \"NVIDIA\" for The Information's report about NVIDIA (the known original-speaker class), not a wiring matter.\n- **Step 2:** 23 records, 20 new groups, 3 repeats, 0 refused, 0 pending, 0 unresolved links.\n- **The ladder step:** the credit-support thesis → a figure ladder (USD, five bands from below $1bn to $30bn and above, the lower end\n  favourable, due 2027-02-28 at the FY2027 report's expected publication); the Ohio thesis → an event ladder (signed by 2027-12-31 against\n  formally abandoned; the amount marked not judged, one bet per question). Both pass the step's checks at the first request; the\n  configuration is the frozen one (`clean-ladder-stage-a d3b4a5f696c7cc82`).\n- **The judge:** credit support at rung 3 ($5bn–$15bn), implied about $10bn ($6.3bn CoreWeave + $3.5bn lease guarantees ≈ $9.8bn, the new\n  model unquantified), every one of the 8 evidence items it cited shown in its request; the Ohio guarantee at rung 3 (unresolved at the due\n  day), reading the $500bn as the asset's cost, not the guarantee. Twelve quotes cut by the judge at 400 characters (its frozen\n  behaviour).\n- **Production:** 13 items, 0 failed; one view (the credit-support amount, due 2027-02-28), judged rung 2 ($1bn–$5bn), anchored on the\n  $3.5bn lease guarantees; its five evidence items do not include the CoreWeave backstop. It did not open the Ohio guarantee. One day,\n  one draw: an observation of where the two systems differ, not a comparison.\n\n## What the slice does not show\n\nThe ladder step's passes 2 and 3; a draw that opens many theses; the judge over many days (the due-day guard, changes, dependencies);\nStage A's and Stage B's readings; any quality line. Those are the full run's (WIRING-DESIGN.md §E).\n# The typed-unit audit (D446): result, 2026-11-13 — the line stops under its own rule\n\n**Answer.** Of the clean path's 16 graded opening losses, the contract's identity fields (`AUDIT-CONTRACT.md`) could detect 2 from the\nstored answers' own values and recover the ruler's question in none; the other 14 are opening choices no identity field exposes.\nUnder the stop rule fixed before the audit (\"if it mostly refuses without a path to recover the question\"; here it mostly cannot\ndetect at all), the typed-unit line stops.\n\n| case | field values the stored thesis supplies | detectable from those values? | refuse or recover | correct theses rejected |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| u1 q05 memory (`clean-5`) | quantity; prices of LPDDR5X, HBM, 3D NAND/DRAM; period \"calendar 2026–2027\" (a span) | yes: a span is neither one year nor a comparison of two named years | refuse only (code cannot choose 2027, 2026 or the comparison) | none at a cost: this thesis got no ladder in Stage A anyway; the D441 neighbours' spans likewise; one-year and comparison theses pass |\n| u2 q09 credit support (`clean-1`) | quantity (\"how much … extended\"); check date 2027-12-31, basis \"within 18 months\" (the unsigned-obligation rule) | yes: an amount question with the signing rule's basis is contradictory | recover the date (an amount: the next annual report), but the scope stays \"AI cloud partners\", a part of q09's total, so the question stays not comparable | none found |\n| u1 q04, u3 q04, u2 q13 (capex years) | the opened year's thesis: quantity, one calendar year (2027 or 2026), a release-date check date | no: the opened thesis is well formed; the missing year has no thesis to check | — (recovering it would mean code opening a question, which the contract forbids) | — |\n| u2 q05, u3 q05 (memory declined) | unavailable: no memory-price thesis | no | — | — |\n| u2 q23, u3 q23 (Ohio folded) | the credit-support amount thesis, well formed; the signing statement linked to it | no from the thesis's fields; `signed: no` would sit on the evidence, and opening the signing question is the model's choice | — | — |\n| u1 q09, u3 q09 (a part of the total) | quantity, scope \"AI cloud partners\", next annual report | no: the scope is a judgement against the ruler's total | — | — |\n| u2 q08, u3 q08 (another event) | event: permission to sell (u2), disclosed sales (u3), a fiscal year-end date | no: a well-formed event; which event to track is judgement | — | — |\n| u1 q18, u3 q18 (Kyber) | event: first shipments \"in 2027 … or delayed to 2028\", check date 2028 (u1); \"in 2028 versus the original 2027\", end 2028 (u3) | no: a date the item gives; whether to ask the planned or the reported date is not settled by the standard (council mm-1) | — | — |\n| u1 q23 (Ohio deadline) | event: signing by 2027-12-31, within 18 months | not a defect (inside the standard's range; the D373 concern) | — | — |\n\nThe correct controls (the agreeing and eligible clean theses: u1 `clean-4`, u3 `clean-6` capex 2027; u2 `clean-4` capex 2026, `clean-5`\nKyber by 2027; the q07 theses) pass every field check. **What the audit shows:** the losses that matter are choices of which question to\nopen (which years, signing beside amount, which event, which scope, whether to open the memory question), the model's judgement that the\ncontract rightly leaves to it; identity fields can only refuse the rare malformed thesis, which Stage A already leaves without a ladder.\nLimits: fields filled from the stored text by me, not by a model under a new schema; one window.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/DECISION-PAGE-2026-11-16.md docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/week/WEEK-RESULT.md'","aggregated_output":"# What waits on Alex: one page for one sitting (2026-11-16, D575)\n\n**In one line:** the new system is not ready to replace the running one.\n\n- The step that writes each question's answer choices still misses your quality gate.\n- The test of its final answers has not passed either.\n- Making it fit into the product has moved a long way.\n\nSix choices are yours, and nothing below decides them for you. Until you choose, the running system stays the default (D544).\n\nThe two systems:\n\n- **The running system** is the pipeline that serves the product today.\n- **The new system** is the rebuilt pipeline (clean Steps 1 to 4). It reads news, opens questions, writes their answer choices and\n  judges them.\n\n## Your choices, in the order they were put to you\n\n### 1. Mark the pilot cards (Deck 1), about half an hour\n\n**What it is:** 19 cards from one stored week, with output from both systems; no card says which system wrote it.\n\n- 12 real answers and 2 planted wrong answers.\n- 2 matters no question took up.\n- 3 sheets of facts that never reached an answer.\n- 3 hidden repeats of earlier cards.\n\n**What you mark:**\n\n- On each answer card: is the question worth tracking (yes / no / cannot tell)? Is the answer supported by what is shown (yes / no /\n  cannot tell)?\n- On the two cards for matters no question took up: only whether the matter is worth tracking (there is no answer to mark).\n- On each fact sheet, for every fact, two marks: does it bear on the question (yes / no / cannot tell)? Would it change the answer,\n  only its confidence, neither, or cannot tell? The sheets include controls.\n- The deck's last box gives your marks as text to paste back; the counting needs the key held outside the repository.\n\n**Why it comes first:** since 2026-11-10 every judgement of quality has come from model readers, and their two families disagree. The\ndeck tests whether your own reading can check them. It ranks neither system and passes no gate.\n\n**Where:** [`pilot-deck-1/deck-1.html`](pilot-deck-1/deck-1.html), opened in a browser.\n\n### 2. What role should your six lines play for the answer-choice step?\n\n**Your six lines (D400):**\n\n1. No set of answer choices asks a different question from its topic.\n2. None leaves a part of its topic unlisted.\n3. None has an outcome with no matching answer, or with two.\n4. No refusal without a justified reason.\n5. At least 35 of the 44 topics right in at least two of three attempts, at least 28 of them with written answer choices.\n6. The same favourable direction in all three attempts for every company figure, and the same due day for at least nine in ten topics.\n\n(The later readings had fewer topics than 44; the council scaled line 5 to them as a diagnostic, not as your line.)\n\n**The record:** the gate has been missed in all nine readings. Not every line failed each time: in the latest reading line 4 was met,\nand line 6 could not be measured there (too few topics of the kinds it covers).\n\n**New (D568, D569):** we read the same 39 answer choices again with fresh readers of the same two model families.\n\n- Every line came out the same as before.\n- The same seven attempts failed line 2.\n\nThat is repeatability under one method. It is not proof that each mark is right.\n\n**Also new:**\n\n- **Line 2's field had no reader.** The parts a question leaves unjudged were shown nowhere until today; they now appear beside each\n  answer.\n- **One reading mark checks a field still unread.** It is not one of your six lines but a mark the readers give (an event's stated plan,\n  part of the reference); that field is read nowhere in the product.\n- **A paper recount:** with both of those marks set aside (the unjudged-parts mark and the event-reference mark), 9 or 7 of 13 topics\n  come out right, against 11 needed. The recount was not done with only the reference mark set aside.\n\n**Options:**\n\n- Keep all six lines, read with every mark, as the gate.\n- Decide separately what role each disputed mark should play (the unjudged-parts mark, now shown on the page; the event-reference\n  mark, read nowhere).\n- Set new lines after your Deck 1 reading.\n\n**Tried since (D577 to D580):** the step was told plainly that the topic's wording means both the description and the outcome.\n\n- In a small test it listed more of the omitted parts.\n- It also declined twice where both readers said it should not.\n\nWe have no reviewed repair. The records do not give a general rule for which part is judged when a topic's outcome names several\nthings (for example, whether a product's volume shipments or its first revenue is the thing tracked). Some of the strict counts\nalso got worse (`unlisted-parts/RESULT.md`).\n\n**Since then (D582 to D588; you were away, so the council decided):**\n- **Your rule added:** a second side of the same bet stays in the question as context (D436). With it (arm A2), the step left no\n  part unlisted on the test topics, and it stopped declining wrongly.\n- **What went wrong instead:** it now also lists things the answer already decides. For example, it lists \"the deal is terminated\"\n  beside a question whose answer choices include exactly that.\n- **One more fix tried (A3):** it removed some of those entries. It added a neighbouring kind. The model readers disagree among\n  themselves on every one of these kinds, and none of them is one of your six lines. So we stopped adjusting this edge.\n- **The test on unread news (D589, D590), the week of 10-13:** the system opened only 5 new questions, too few independent ones\n  for a verdict, so the comparison closed without one. On those 5 questions:\n  - the current step still leaves a part unlisted on one question (your line 2);\n  - on another, it lets \"Ruled out\" catch a mere postponement (your line 3);\n  - its refusals were all justified (your line 4).\n  - A2 did no worse anywhere, and A2 is still not accepted.\n- **The two model reader families disagree on the due day of company figures in annual reports.** Your rule (D414) sets that day\n  in code. Read alone, both families now apply your rule, so the reader instructions were left unchanged.\n- **A new question for you (D591): when a company announces that something will happen later than a question's date, is the\n  question \"ruled out\" or \"still open\"?**\n  - **The example:** \"Will Vera Rubin start shipping by 31 December 2026?\" Nvidia announces that first shipments move to 2027.\n  - **The current step writes this as \"ruled out\"** on 37 stored answers, and the model readers mostly accept it.\n  - **For a deal's closing or a government's rules moved past the date, the readers lean to \"still open\".** No rule of yours\n    covers the difference.\n  - **Option 1, \"ruled out\" (recommended, not your existing rule):** a deadline bet counts as lost when the one who decides\n    announces a slip past the date. Cost: on the card, a delay and a cancellation look the same.\n  - **Option 2, \"still open\":** the card shows no decision, and the question closes without the event having happened. Cost: about\n    37 stored answers become failures of your line 3, and the step needs a repair.\n  - **Until you choose:** nothing changes, and the 38 affected answers are listed beside every gate count\n    (`unlisted-parts/fresh-a2/postponement/BOUNDARY-CASES.md`).\n- **The final-answer test (D592):** it can compare only 4 questions with your reference where 8 are needed. No single repair the\n  council can make reaches 8: delivering three missed facts reaches 7, and the eighth needs changes to two steps at once. A new\n  reference set would be your choice.\n- **Running the new system live (D593; your choice 6):** the rules a live database must follow were written and agreed after nine\n  rounds of review: how readers see one consistent day, what happens on a crash or a full disk, and who may copy it. Next they are\n  proven at no cost on test databases. Nothing runs live, and the one exception to the working rule \"never open a live run's\n  databases\" is only proposed, for you.\n\n**Until you choose:** the gate stays as written and stays unmet.\n\n### 3. What should the next product test start from? (D559)\n\n**The choice:**\n\n- **Option A:** confirm that the next product-oriented test starts from a real library of questions already held, built by an earlier\n  run. Tests that start from empty can stay as diagnostics.\n- **Option B:** choose another starting condition.\n\n**Evidence, with its limits (D561 to D564):**\n\n- **Small comparison, 11 articles:** read once from a held library and once from empty. The held library opened 4 new questions where\n  the empty start opened 16. It did not open a separate equity-commitments question that the empty start opened; those facts went under\n  a held question, and both reader families accepted that home. By the rule written before the run, the result fell between its two\n  outcomes.\n- **The fortnight run, one draw:** week 2's six new questions repeated none of the held ones. This cannot show the problem is solved in\n  general.\n\n**Until you choose:** each test states its own starting condition.\n\n### 4. Answers placed with nothing behind them (D520)\n\n**The problem has two cases, which need not get the same answer:**\n\n- **Case one:** nothing shown to the judge sizes the amount, yet it names a band.\n- **Case two:** the estimate rests on an unsupported step the judge supplied itself (a discount, a growth rate), and that step decides\n  the band. (A step the judge supplies is not wrong in itself; the case is the unsupported one.)\n\n**Options for each case:**\n\n- Show the band as today.\n- Show it with a plain label of what it rests on.\n- Show no band.\n\nNo recommendation is recorded among these.\n\n**What was tried (D565, D566):**\n\n- **Asking the judge:** asked after the fact which band its own evidence sized, the judge did not separate the answers readers had\n  failed from those they had passed.\n- **Two reader families:** they mostly agreed on the narrower mark that no amount was the answer's starting point, and split on\n  whether a scaled comparable is a basis. That narrower observation came after the fact, and the check's own verdict was \"unclear\".\n\nNeither a label nor a \"no band\" rule has been built or tested.\n\n**Until you choose:** the band is shown, as today.\n\n### 5. Two directions the running product invents (D571); two separate choices\n\n**5a. Yes/No questions:** \"Yes\" is coloured as good news and \"No\" as bad. A \"Yes\" can be bad for the company. Change it, or keep it.\n\n**5b. Theme ranges:** the highest range is scored +2 by inference. For a theme such as memory prices, higher may be bad. Change it,\nor keep it.\n\n**What the new system does instead:**\n\n- Its events, and its theme ranges with no registered favourable direction, are shown in neutral colours, with no score.\n- An Idea's combined score is not computed when one of these is among its drivers, and the Idea card says which one.\n\n### 6. Running the new system live\n\n**Where it stands now (D594 to D603):** the design was resumed under D439, agreed, built and tested on test copies at no cost. Nothing\nis running live and nothing has been restarted.\n\n**What the tests show:**\n\n- While the new system writes a day, the website and the agent tools only ever show whole finished days.\n- On a brand-new database's first day, they say \"not ready yet\" instead of showing a half-done day (open defect 10 is fixed).\n- Only one writer can work on a database at a time.\n- A copy for checking is taken safely, and a crash or a full disk loses nothing that was already finished.\n\n**Example:** the test re-ran six months of one draw while asking the website for the library about 170 times. Every answer was a\nwhole finished day, never part of one.\n\n**Three choices are yours:**\n\n1. **How live data is read.** Today's working rule never opens a running database directly, only a copy (AGENTS §9). The product's\n   own readers would need an exception (P1). This is proposed, not decided.\n2. **Restarting the running service.** It picks up the new behaviour only on a restart. Example: a date after the last finished day\n   would then be refused instead of answered from a half-done day.\n3. **When to adopt.** Adopting the new system at all still comes after the quality gates.\n\n**One small gap remains your choice:** if the page cannot reach the service, it quietly keeps the copy built into the page, as\nspecified. A \"collected snapshot\" note is a possible change.\n\n## What is in force meanwhile\n\n- **The product:** the running system serves it, unchanged.\n- **The council:** works under D439, deciding reversibly and recording each decision. Any paid send needs its own decision, within the\n  $40 ceiling; about $26.18 is used.\n- **The new system's integration, checked on stored replays and serving tests:**\n  - the page shows its questions, including the parts each question does not judge, with no invented direction;\n  - due questions close on their due day, as in the running system;\n  - a freshly opened page shows the latest completed day.\n  - Still open: the first-day defect, the live-service design and the Idea planner on a new-system database.\n- **The final-answer test (D576):** it could compare only four questions with the reference, because the other questions, or the\n  facts shown to the judge, did not meet its control conditions; eight were required. This says nothing either way about the judge on\n  the excluded questions. Re-running the judge cannot change it.\n- **Next, at no cost (D576):** a count of the answer choices that leave a part of the question unlisted (your line 2), now that the\n  page shows that list; any change is brought to the council before it is tried.\n\n## Where the evidence is\n\n- **The record of this stretch:** decisions D559 to D575 (`docs/DECISIONS-log.md`).\n- **The round log:** §§53 to 57 (`LEAN-LOG.md`).\n- **The page and serving checks:** `page-contract/` (`RESULT.md`, `SERVE-RESULT.md`, `SERVING-WHILE-WRITING-RESULT.md`).\n- **The repeated reading:** `gate1-stick/RESULT.md`.\n- **The fortnight run:** `carried-run/RESULT.md`.\n- **The earlier page:** `DECISION-PAGE-2026-11-15.md`.\n# Lean step 2: one fresh week, one draw a system — result (D487, D488), 2026-11-14\n\n**Answer: by the rule written before reading, the difference is not clear.** Production covers two more of the five big matters both reader\nfamilies listed (4 against 2), which meets the rule's first half; its share of last judgements both families call supported is lower by 19\npoints (10 of 15 against 6 of 7), more than the one in ten the second half allows. Each half has one named cause. The first is one rule: the\nthree matters the clean path lacks are the company's own revenue and margin, which its request forbids as questions (D382). The second rests\nmostly on one reader family: Codex failed 2 of the 4 judgements a separate reader had confirmed as supported, both of them production's;\nboth families call only one judgement unsupported, production's data-center revenue.\n\nMaterial: 2026-09-22..28, 235 articles (232 never sent by any run), each system one draw from an empty library: the clean path\n`poc/data/clean-lean-week-2026-11-14/week-r2/c1` (after D488), production `…/week/p1`. Readers: Codex (read-only) and Claude subagents, blind\nto the systems and to each other; the keys are outside the repository. Model cost of the step (the one-day slice, production's draw, the\nclean draw and its two stopped attempts): $4.69. One draw each: every count below is one draw.\n\n## What each system holds at the week's end (counted from the stored runs)\n\n| | clean c1 | production p1 |\n|---|---|---|\n| questions opened | 7 | 15 |\n| statements written | 569, from 182 articles | 673, from 186 articles |\n| thrown away for having no question | 0 | 278 |\n| kept (after joining repeats) | 569 (314) | 385 |\n| kept with no question: no judgement reads them | 389 | 0 |\n| links to questions | 196 | 449 (126 on FY2027 total revenue, 94 on data-center revenue) |\n| judgements | 37 | 72 |\n\n## A. Big questions (40 sampled articles; eight matters, five listed by both families)\n\n| matter | listed by | clean c1 | production p1 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| M01 FY2027 revenue (total or data center) | both | **missed** | opened (v2, v4) |\n| M02 FY2028 revenue | both | **missed** | opened (v3, v5) |\n| M03 FY2028 gross margin against the 72%–73% outlook | both | **missed** | opened (v10) |\n| M04 supply and capacity purchase commitments ($279B at Q2) | both | opened (clean-2) | **missed** |\n| M05 Beijing's approval of RTX Pro 5500 purchases | both | opened (clean-7) | opened (v14, v15) |\n| M06 FY2027 gross margin | one | missed | missed |\n| M07 Vera Rubin revenue shipments in Q3 | one | opened (Claude) / a later deadline that does not settle Q3 (Codex) | the same split |\n| M08 AMD's 2027 data-center revenue | one | missed | missed |\n\nThe families give the same status on 14 of 16 cells and on all ten cells of M01–M05. **Covered, of the five: clean 2, production 4.**\n\nEach opened question, read beside the article it was opened from:\n\n| | clean c1 (7) | production p1 (15) |\n|---|---|---|\n| worth holding | 6 (both families) | 9 (Codex) / 8 (Claude) |\n| trivia | 1: the Hugging Face purchase closing | 3: \"tech companies' AI spending\" (nothing settles it), the Hugging Face price as a commitment, the Anthropic IPO stake |\n| duplicate of another of its own | 0 | 3 by both: FY2028 growth of FY2028 revenue, Rubin by end-2027 of Rubin by January 2027, the second RTX Pro 5500 question; Claude adds data-center revenue of total revenue |\n\nChecked by Claude against the stored runs:\n- Production has no supply-commitments question: its one kept statement on the $279 billion is linked to the credit-support question (v1).\n- The clean path's three misses are its rule at work. On each article where production opened v2, v3, v4, v5 and v10, the clean draw's\n  stored reasoning names the request's sentence \"Do not open a thesis about … a financial measure's level, growth, margin or guidance for\n  any period, on its own\", opens nothing and keeps the statements; 109 of 235 steps' reasoning names that sentence. The request sends such\n  figures to \"an open thesis about its revenue or earnings path\", which nothing in an empty library is: 145 kept statements of the company's\n  own figures (from 67 articles) have no question, among them the 70% growth outlook for fiscal 2028, which the articles set against the 45%\n  the Street had modelled (14 statements, 14 articles), and gross-margin figures of 71% to 73% (11 statements, 9 articles).\n- Both families call the Hugging Face purchase small (both systems opened a question on it). It is listed as the readers gave it; D389's\n  size line is for unsigned obligations in its own test and does not rule on this question (council round 11).\n\n## B. Judgement support (26 items: 22 real last judgements, 4 planted from earlier judgements)\n\n| | Codex | Claude |\n|---|---|---|\n| planted wrong judgements caught | 4 of 4 | 4 of 4 |\n| supported controls passed (confirmed first by a separate reader) | **2 of 4** | 4 of 4 |\n| clean c1 (7): supported / unsupported / insufficient | 6 / 1 / 0 | 7 / 0 / 0 |\n| production p1 (15) | 10 / 2 / 3 | 14 / 1 / 0 |\n\nSupported by both families: **clean 6 of 7 (86%), production 10 of 15 (67%)**. Unsupported by both: one, production's data-center revenue (v4):\nits rung is reached by a fourth-quarter growth of about 25% that the judge itself calls an allowance; no shown piece gives the rate, and the\nshown 18% lands a rung lower. Called not supported by Codex alone: the clean path's credit-support total (a cap read as the amount);\nproduction's credit-support total and its first RTX Pro 5500 question (**both are confirmed supported controls**), the Hugging Face amount and\nthe Anthropic stake (both also called trivia in A). Cited statements with a field error: one in each system (a quarter mislabelled; \"more than\ndoubling to $115 billion\" written as \"will exceed\").\n\n## The rule, applied as written (LEAN-LOG §2)\n\n- First half (A): production covers two more of the matters both families listed. Met by production.\n- Second half (B): its share of judgements both families call supported is lower by 19 points, more than one in ten. Not met.\n- **Verdict: not clear. The next small step targets the largest difference observed, not a choice of system.**\n\nWhat weakens each half: A's three-matter gap is one sentence of the clean request, a product rule (D382), not a capability. B compares 7\njudgements with 15, so one judgement is 14 points for the clean path; the gap comes from one family, which parted from the separate reader on two of the four supported controls, both production items, with\nreasons that stand as a disagreement (a guarantee cap read as the amount; permission for two named firms read from \"some firms\"). As a\nsensitivity note only: with those two counted as the separate reader read them, production would stand at 12 of 15; the rule's count stays\n10 of 15. B at this size cannot say which system judges better.\n\n## The largest difference observed\n\nHow each system treats the company's own revenue and margin: three of the five matters, 145 kept statements no judgement reads on one side;\non the other, the same matters opened, with two or three duplicates among them and 126 statements piled on one question.\n\n## The source check the council asked for ($0, council round 11): do the articles hold a dispute, or only a number?\n\nClaude's first candidate for a next step was a narrow door on the clean path: open a revenue or margin question only when the item holds both\nthe company's own outlook and a stated market expectation for the same measure and period. Read against every article the readers listed\nfor M01–M03 and every article production opened its five such questions from:\n\n| matter | articles | hold such a pair | what they hold instead |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| M01 FY2027 revenue | 7 | **0** | the consensus alone ($406 billion, $412 billion, \"about 90% growth\"), or the company's guide for one quarter ($108 billion); the company gives no outlook for the year |\n| M02 FY2028 revenue | 8 | 2 with figures (70% guided \"against the 45% the Street had modelled\", a gap already closed: the consensus is now about $672 billion), 1 in words | the 70% outlook alone (5 articles) |\n| M03 FY2028 gross margin | 2 | 1 (the 72%–73% outlook, \"analysts assume margins hold roughly steady\", memory prices above what management expected) | the outlook alone |\n\n- **The pair does not cover the gap** (it never opens FY2027 revenue): the candidate is dropped, as its falsifier said.\n- **Why the readers listed these matters:** every one of their 24 revenue and margin entries gives the reason \"main driver\" (the company's\n  main number for the year), not a dispute. That is the standard's own question type, which D384 leaves out of the clean path and the\n  frozen question gold marks optional (may_open).\n- **A counter-example on production's side:** its data-center revenue question (v4) was opened from an article that holds only last\n  quarter's reported figure (\"$89 billion of data-center revenue out of $96.2 billion\"), no outlook and no expectation. It is the question\n  one family calls a duplicate of total revenue, and its judgement is the one both families call unsupported.\n- **So the choice is the plain one, and it is Alex's:** whether the new system holds questions on the company's main numbers at all\n  (D382, D384). The week shows what each answer costs; it does not show a narrower rule that would get both.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
