{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/TRACE.md'","aggregated_output":"# The clean path's 17 graded opening losses, traced (D445), 2026-11-13\n\nEach case followed along its path (article and request → raw answer → checks → opened thesis → matching → eligibility), from the stored\ndraws u1–u3 (`poc/data/clean-unseeded-2026-11-10/`), the matching readers (`clean-arch/unseeded-2026-11-10/match-u*`), the Stage B\ncontrol readers (`poc/data/clean-stage-b-reading-2026-11-12/controls-answers/`), the Step 1 diagnosis (`clean-arch/step1-diagnosis-\n2026-11-12/DIAGNOSIS.md`, its table of the nine unopened questions, with the model's own accounts) and, for memory, the two articles that\ncarry reader B's decisive items (i252, i254, i255: gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e, 07-07; i142: gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32, 07-06).\n\"Standard\" marks whether the product's question breaches the frozen standard, is reasonable but not comparable with the ruler's, or is\nnot settled by the standard.\n\n| draw, question | outcome | differences observed | first point the ruler's question is lost | standard |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| u1 q04, u3 q04, u2 q13 (capex years) | not opened | one capex thesis for one year, the other year its base or benchmark | opening, 2026-07-04 article (step 42): the merge sentences beat the year unit (the model's accounts) | breach (D38: one view a year) |\n| u1 q05 (memory) | not opened | scope wider (NAND, LPDDR5X, per-rack cost); period a 2026–2027 span | opening, the memory article (step 56); the decisive facts were then extracted and linked to it (07-06 ev-8, ev-9; 07-07 ev1, ev2 → clean-5) | breach (\"one quantity for one period\"; the model: \"a slip\") |\n| u2 q05, u3 q05 (memory) | not opened | no memory-price thesis; HBM capacity opened at step 165 instead | opening, steps 56, 63 and 90: the decisive facts extracted and left unlinked; the model declines a memory-price thesis: \"no specific price/date; tangential for NVDA; the article is Micron-centric\" (u2), \"the item doesn't give a specific contract price series with a release date\" (u3) | breach in the reason given (a shared quantity's due day is its release date, which the rule fixes, not the article); whether to open is the model's judgement |\n| u2 q23, u3 q23 (Ohio) | not opened | the signing statement linked to the credit-support amount thesis | opening: \"Signing the OpenAI guarantee is a component of that amount\" (u2); \"the scoping rule wins\" (u3) | breach (an unsigned obligation is a yes/no question beside the amount, §3 C) |\n| u1 q23 (Ohio) | not eligible | deadline 2027-12-31 against 2027-06-30 | eligibility (D430) | reasonable, not comparable (the standard allows a check date within 18 months; D373 concern) |\n| u1 q09, u3 q09 (financing commitments) | not eligible | a part: credit support to AI cloud partners against all financing commitments and backstops | opening (scope) | uncertain (D248 boundary 1 makes the plan's forms one question; the control trace leaves open whether the narrower programme is a product error or a consequence of the broad matching rule) |\n| u2 q09 | not opened | a 2027-12-31 check date for an undertaken amount | opening (the unsigned-obligation date applied to an undertaken amount) | breach (an undertaken amount is tracked at the next annual report) |\n| u2 q08, u3 q08 (China H200) | not eligible | another event: permission to sell (u2), NVIDIA's disclosed sales (u3), against the authorities' authorization to import | opening (event) | reasonable, not comparable (another question about the same matter) |\n| u1 q08 | unresolved | \"a Chinese AI firm\" against \"any Chinese company\" | eligibility: the families split | — |\n| u1 q18 (Kyber) | not eligible | shipments \"during calendar 2027\" with a due day of 2028-12-31 | the due day | not settled by the standard (the event's window is the ruler's; its due day is a year late) |\n| u3 q18 | not eligible | first shipments \"during calendar 2028\" (the reported slip) | opening (the slipped year as the question) | not settled by the standard (D248 boundary 2 governs an open earlier question; none was open) |\n\n**Mechanisms by count (16 losses, 1 unresolved):** the year unit lost to the merge sentences 3; memory 3 (shape 1, the price question\ndeclined 2); an unsigned obligation folded into an amount 2; a part of a total 2; another event of the same matter 2; another deadline\nor due day 3 (one a ruler concern); a date rule misapplied 1. **No single mechanism explains most losses:** each graded question fails\nby its own route, two or three cases each. Breaches of a rule the frozen standard states, reconciled case by case: **7 certain** (the year unit 3; one quantity for one period,\nu1 q05; signing beside amount, u2 q23 and u3 q23; the undertaken-amount date, u2 q09) and **2 uncertain** (u1 q09 and u3 q09, a part of\nthe financing total: the control trace, `stage_b/CONTROL_TRACE.md`, leaves open whether the narrower programme is a product error or a\nconsequence of the broad matching rule); the two memory declines give a reason the standard does not support, but the opening decision\nitself is the model's judgement and is not counted. The rest are reasonable questions the ruler does not compare, or are not settled by\nthe standard. Two earlier sentence-level attempts on these very classes failed or moved their\nerror (the year change, D441; the Ohio line, D391).\n\n**For q05, the gate-2 diagnostic beside it (not an end-to-end rate):** on the memory article alone, c8 opened a memory-price thesis in\n28 of 54 sends, 13 of them in the ruler's shape by my marks (D441); the stored draws opened it in the wrong shape once and not at all twice.\n\n**Limits.** 17 cases over three draws of one window; the model's accounts exist for the unopened cases only (DIAGNOSIS.md); the matching\nand eligibility readers split along family lines on several cases.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/unlisted-parts/fresh-a2/line5/RESULT.md'","aggregated_output":"# Why the fresh topics miss line 5 (D604's trace), 2026-11-16, $0\n\n`line5_trace.py` → `line5-trace-output.txt`. It reads the stored fresh reading (`poc/data/clean-fresh-a2-2026-11-16/choices/reading`)\nwith the gate-1 scorer's own functions, for the control arm (the frozen step). It reproduces the recorded line 5: 2 of 5 right with\nunresolved points counted for the answer, 1 of 5 with them counted against.\n\n## Topic by topic\n\n| Topic (kind) | What blocks it, counted for / against | How settled | Reaches what a reader of the product sees? | Existing record |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| clean-14 (theme figure) | XR / XR | Passes 1 and 3: both third readers fail XR (the reference: IDC's \"19% growth\" forecast for a unit count). Pass 2 split. | **Not in these cases** (corrected in round 146, see below). | Event reference has no bounded repair (D557, D558); \"what counts as an expectation\" is an open form issue. |\n| clean-15 (theme figure) | L, XR / L, XR | L: pass 1 both first readers; pass 2 both third readers; pass 3 split. XR: pass 1 both first readers. | **Yes, for L:** a part the question leaves out, now shown in the card's list of parts not judged. XR: the same visible choice either way (below). | Line 2's class; no sourced main-part rule (D580); tuning stopped (D586, D391). |\n| clean-16 (AI bill) | none / none: three justified refusals | Both families: N holds. | Right. | |\n| clean-17 (event) | R, XR / R, XR | R: pass 3 both third readers (the wait-as-ruling-out class); pass 1 split. XR: all three passes, both first readers. | **Yes, for R:** the Ruled-out rung's words. XR: no (the judge never sees an event's reference, D557). | The postponement boundary, on Alex's page (D591). |\n| clean-18 (company figure) | none / D | D split on all three passes (Codex fails, Claude holds). | **Yes,** the due day is shown, and it is code's D414 day, 60 days after year-end. Readers who fail it read the request's fiscal-year note (\"around 2027-02; use that day\"), which contradicts code. | The reader discrepancy (D590): read alone, both families hold D on the class. The request-and-code contradiction (`universe.py:136` against D414) is recorded and not changed. |\n\n## What this gives\n\n- **On fresh topics, line 5's misses reduce to four recorded things:**\n  - reference choices (XR, on 3 of 4 written topics, and the only blocker of clean-14). In these cases a rule-following answer would\n    produce the same answer choices; see the section below;\n  - a stopped class (L);\n  - an unresolved policy boundary (R, Alex's);\n  - a reader discrepancy on Alex's own rule (D).\n- **No common, sourced product mechanism is found.** By D604's rule this closes the route to a candidate. The gate stays unmet; a\n  later candidate is not excluded.\n- **A diagnostic recount of the stored marks, not a gate result and not a change of any line.** Without XR, line 5 on the fresh\n  topics would read 3 of 5 (unresolved for) or 2 of 5 (against). The development map's 9 or 7 of 13 (`gate1-stick/RESULT.md`) was\n  counted without both L and XR, not without XR alone.\n- **Limits:** one week, one draw, 5 topics, control arm only.\n\n## Corrected in round 146, and the source-backed reading (D604's unfinished part)\n\n**My error.** I wrote that no product reader sees the ladder's reference. That is wrong for figures. `ladder_step.write_view` puts the\nreference into the middle answer choice's words, which the judge is shown and the page carries:\n- with a stated number, \"(the reference when the question opened: \"…\")\";\n- with a last reported amount, \"(the amount last reported …)\";\n- otherwise \"(no reference figure when the question opened)\".\n\nAn event's reference is used by no rung. I had relied on an older finding and a search for the word in `bundle.py`, without following\nthe writer.\n\n**Each XR failure, read beside its article.** The articles are in `cases/cases.json`; the readers' reasons are in\n`choices/reading/third-reader` and `readers-answers`.\n\n| Topic, passes | What the article states | The answer's reference | Readers | What the judge and page see |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| clean-14, all three | \"China's broader AI accelerator market reached about 2.008 million units in the first half … IDC expects total accelerator shipments to grow 19% in 2026\". No full-year 2025 count, so no expected count is stated. | null | Claude: the 19% is an expectation for this very quantity, so it should be quoted (kind `expected`, no number). Codex: a growth rate, not a count, so null holds in packet 1 and fails in packet 2. | **The same either way.** With no number in the figure's unit, a quoted words-only reference gives the same middle choice as null: \"(no reference figure when the question opened)\". |\n| clean-15, pass 1 | Microsoft: \"Starting next calendar year, we will scale our Maia family to multi-gigawatt deployments\". | null | Both first readers fail, so it was settled without a third reader. On passes 2 and 3, and on the A2 arm, both families' third readers rule that a null reference is right, because it is one company's plan, not an expectation for the combined capacity. | The same either way, since no number. The fail is a reading inconsistency across passes. |\n| clean-17, all three (event) | \"Taiwan will tighten export controls on high-end AI processors and servers, mirroring U.S. rules\". | null | Both first readers fail: an expected event belongs in the reference. | **Nothing:** an event's reference is used by no rung. |\n\n**What it gives:**\n\n- **Every XR failure on these topics leaves what the judge and the page are shown unchanged.** An answer that followed the rule\n  exactly would produce the same answer choices: a words-only reference for clean-14 and clean-17, or null for clean-15.\n  - The rule allows \"no number the article does not state\".\n  - None of these articles states a number in the figure's unit.\n  - An event's reference never reaches a rung.\n- **The disputes are about the rule's reach:** whether a growth rate counts as an expectation for a unit count, and whether one\n  company's plan does for a combined figure. \"What counts as an expectation\" is the open form issue already recorded.\n- **The narrower claim (round 147):** this checks the writer's behaviour on the stored answers; it is not a test that a new model\n  request would leave every other field unchanged.\n- **Under D604 and round 146's criterion this closes the route.** It found disputed reference choices with no product consequence and\n  no sourced general mechanism. The gate stays unmet; no paid work follows without a new candidate.\n- **Observation, outside the line marks:**\n  - A Claude first reader's G remark on clean-14: the middle range (3.5 to 4.0 million) sits below the first half's annual rate\n    (about 4.0 million) and below what IDC's growth implies.\n  - G is not one of the line marks.\n  - The ranges are product-visible, but one reader of one family on one topic is a lead, not a class.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1310,1437p' docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/LEAN-LOG.md\"","aggregated_output":"  registered sample after it missed its minimum.\n- **The fresh gate-1 reading of the frozen step** (5 topics, 30 sends, $0.0282; `unlisted-parts/RESULT-FRESH-A2.md`):\n  - line 2 missed (the custom-silicon topic);\n  - line 3 missed (a Ruled-out rung that catches a postponement);\n  - line 4 met;\n  - line 5: 2 or 1 of 5 right.\n- **A2:** better or equal everywhere, with no new agreed fault, but no verdict. The comparison is STOPPED: a Codex first reader\n  missed plant 1, the control fails L on 1 topic, and the countings disagree. A2 stays unaccepted.\n- **The two families' third readers split on 21 of 29 points.** The largest share is the due-day mark (D).\n  - **Census over 14 stored readings:** 225 company figures show a code-set day that differs from the fiscal-year note's month.\n    Codex first readers fail D on 133, Claude on 0.\n  - **Read reason by reason:** 128 are obligation figures where Codex dates \"the next annual filing\" by the note's \"around\n    February\". Alex's D414 dates it by code (60 days after year-end), and he accepted the month's lateness in his own words.\n  - **The other 5** are a real error (a calendar-2027 question labelled FY2028) that the Claude family passed.\n- **My errors caught:**\n  - A line-6 lead read from the model's raw due day instead of the stored view that code sets.\n  - A first brief sentence for D that could have overridden stated days and the obligation clause. The council refused it\n    (round 103), and the cause was then read from the reasons, not inferred from the census's association.\n- **Lessons:**\n  1. Read the stored field the product uses before calling a send inconsistent.\n  2. A census selector shows association. The cause is read from the reasons, and a fix is written to the rule the reasons cite.\n  3. Before proposing a rule, search the decisions for Alex's own answer: D414 already settled the question the readers split on.\n- **The D diagnostic (council rounds 103 to 106; `unlisted-parts/fresh-a2/d-diagnostic/`):**\n  - The council refused it three times before any reader:\n    - my first sentence could override stated days and the obligation clause;\n    - the sample repeated topics across groups;\n    - it had no stated-day control for an obligation.\n  - Built as frozen, it held 24 distinct topics, 2 of them constructed controls, with expectations checked by a fresh checker.\n  - Read D-only, fresh readers of both families hold all 12 class items under both briefs. The sentence changes nothing and is not\n    adopted.\n  - Recorded as a reader discrepancy (round 107). The brief is unchanged.\n- **Next (D590):** a $0 census of Ruled-out rungs that catch a postponement (line 3), screen validated, answers and topics counted.\n- **Lesson 4:** a reader split that a single-mark reading does not reproduce is not fixed by rewording the rule. Test the rule with\n  frozen controls in both directions before writing it into the form.\n\n## 64. Ruled-out rungs that catch a postponement: one authority miss, 37 company delays, and a boundary no decision settles (D591), $0.0007\n\n- **The census** (`unlisted-parts/fresh-a2/postponement/`):\n  - The keyword screen was too wide (104 of its 135 picks passed by both families) and missed \"during\" forms, so counts came from\n    the stored marks.\n  - In 296 frozen-step event answers, the Ruled-out first sentence is a timing statement in 37 company delays (both families pass\n    R on 29) and in 1 authority case (Taiwan p3, failed by both third readers).\n  - The day-passing kind D416 fixed is gone from the frozen step.\n- **The model's own account of Taiwan p3:** it had no named proposal to call withdrawn, so it carried the question's deadline into\n  the decision.\n- **Worked cases:** five, a positive and two counterexamples among them, with each card's wording under each reading. The readers\n  split by decider, with no rule behind the split.\n- **Decided (rounds 108, 109):** no change. Both readings are recorded, the 38 answers are listed as candidates affected, and the\n  question goes on Alex's page with a labelled lean. Next is the final-answer test's control shortfall, as a $0 design.\n- **My error caught:** I first leaned to writing reading 1 into the brief. That would have turned the step's one fresh line-3\n  failure into a pass by changing the form: relaxing the ruler on reader judgment. I withdrew it before the council saw it.\n- **Lesson:** when the cheapest fix of a failure is a change to the measuring form, ask first whether the change would pass the very\n  answer it was found on. If so, it goes to Alex as a question, not into the form.\n\n## 65. The final-answer test's shortfall stated; the live store's operating contract agreed in nine versions (D592, D593), $0\n\n- **The shortfall:** an unchanged re-send stays at 4 of 8 comparable controls; delivering three facts gives at most 7; the eighth\n  needs an unproven two-step change. The 7-control course is not started for the line's sake.\n- **The operating contract:** resumed under D439 after D575's pause, and refused eight times before it was agreed (rounds 111 to 119).\n  Each round found real gaps:\n  - `to_day` bounds;\n  - WAL size and busy reads;\n  - committed partial-day writes;\n  - start-up indexing sending unfinished views outside;\n  - full-text ranking statistics;\n  - the copy's authority;\n  - MCP's shared connection;\n  - index retries;\n  - store identity on replacement;\n  - lock aliases, start races and the lock folder's lifecycle;\n  - a path swapped around an open;\n  - hard links and WAL side files.\n- **Version 9 is agreed for a $0 proof build on test stores.** Live operation is not authorized, and P1 stays proposed.\n- **Lessons:**\n  1. A live-data contract has to name every reader, copier and date bound from the code, not from the route list.\n  2. Each guarantee needs either a mechanism or an honest limit; claims of \"never\" fell to counterexamples four times.\n  3. Narrowing a contract to a stated precondition is a legitimate answer when a binding cannot be built.\n\n## 66. The live store's contract built and proven in slices at $0 (D594 to D603)\n\n- **Built** (uncommitted; nothing restarted):\n  - one owner for every date bound;\n  - one pinned read per request, through `service.open_pinned`, which names the store on failure;\n  - search over visible views only;\n  - the writer's WAL store, its stops, lock and end;\n  - preflight's locked copy and the snapshot route;\n  - every reader opening a store by its own path.\n- **Proven:** serving while the built writer runs (proof 10, 23 of 23); the page's day reaching the Idea call (2f); and the crash,\n  disk and busy proofs (5a, 5b, 6a, 6b, 7′, 8′). The suite passes (346), and so do the new system's folders (192). Mutants: 24 for\n  4b, 6 for slice 5, and every one caught.\n- **Real findings fixed on the way:**\n  - a WAL store without side files cannot be opened read-only, so the writer leaves WAL on every exit;\n  - preflight read each store a second time unlocked;\n  - the API and MCP opened hard-linked stores;\n  - a busy result and other SQLite failures were reported alike.\n- **Not understood, and stated as such:** SQLite's disk I/O error on a renamed store appears only after another test has run in the\n  same process. A renamed store breaks the contract's precondition anyway.\n- **My errors:**\n  - tests written before reading the functions they drive (`acquire` calls `verify`, a nonexistent `view_switches.day`, the\n    library's 24-card page, `rowid`);\n  - one disk image shared by two variants;\n  - the wrong store chosen for a stored card;\n  - a loose \"both sides\" count that I first reported as evidence.\n- **Lessons:**\n  1. Read the schema and the function before the assertion. Every wasted round here was a check written blind.\n  2. A combined-run-only failure is traced through the recorded traceback before any change. It was a real ordering issue in the\n     writer's own reporting.\n  3. When a stand-in is used (the exclusive lock for WAL busy reads), first prove that it exercises the condition, then call it a\n     stand-in in the record.\n\n## 67. Re-ranked from the goal; line 5's fresh misses traced and the route closed (D604, D605), $0\n\n- **The re-rank (round 145):** with the live store proven, Step 4's Gate 1 is the largest blocker. The council chose a $0 trace of\n  the fresh line-5 misses before any candidate. Stage B's eighth control was not chosen first.\n- **The trace:** it reproduces 2 of 5 and 1 of 5. Each topic's blocking marks:\n  - clean-14: XR;\n  - clean-15: L and XR;\n  - clean-17: R and XR;\n  - clean-18: D, counted against only.\n- **The source-backed reading:** in every XR failure, a rule-following answer gives the same answer choices, because a figure shows\n  a reference only with a number in its unit and an event's reference reaches no rung.\n- **Closed, no candidate:** the gate stays unmet, and paid work is held until new evidence or Alex's rulings.\n- **My errors:**\n  - I claimed the reference reaches no reader, from an older finding and a word search, without following the writer. The council\n    caught it.\n  - I attributed the development recount to XR alone, when it was counted without both L and XR.\n- **Lesson:** \"who reads this field\" is answered by following the writer into what it renders (rung text), not by searching the\n  readers for the field's name.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/unlisted-parts/fresh-a2/postponement/AUDIT-RESULT.md docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/unlisted-parts/fresh-a2/fiscal-note/RESULT.md'","aggregated_output":"# The postponed-event class, audited (D606's step), 2026-11-16, $0\n\n`audit_collect.py` → `audit-answers.jsonl`: every event answer the frozen answer-choice step wrote in the stored gate-1 readings.\n- Probe readings contribute their control sends only, by their sealed keys; changed arms, plants, and runs 2 and 3 before the frozen\n  step are left out.\n- That gives 246 answers shown, 195 distinct, over 43 topics.\n- The census's \"296\" counted more readings' answers; this audit uses only the frozen step's own, which is the narrower base.\n\n`audit-screen.txt` and `audit-class.txt` hold the timing ruling-outs, picked by a narrow screen: \"will not … by/during\", postpone,\ndefer, delay, \"moved … to after\". A sample of 12 unpicked decisions found one missed form (\"moved … to after\"), now added. The\nresult is 34 distinct answers (45 shown) over 7 matters, each read whole, with its rungs and every first and third reader's R and\nreason.\n\n## The matters\n\nCounts are from one grouping, by the question's words (`audit-counts.txt`). An earlier table here was from a coarser grouping and\ndid not add up (council round 149).\n\n| Matter (decider) | Distinct answers | Shown | First readers' R on the answers shown | Readers' reasons for a fail |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Vera Rubin first shipments by 2026-12-31 (NVIDIA) | 10 | 15 | 12 both hold; 3 split (third readers: 2 both hold, 1 split) | \"a delay rather than a decision against first shipments as a whole\" |\n| Vera Rubin volume shipments and revenue in FY2027 (NVIDIA) | 9 | 11 | 9 both hold, 1 split, 1 both fail | \"can be a forecast of delay rather than a decision against the event\", plus a W about the 10-K's day |\n| Kyber NVL144 / Rubin Ultra first shipments (NVIDIA) | 8 | 11 | 9 both hold, 2 split | **another class:** the question asks \"during 2027\" but Yes says \"by 2027-12-31\" |\n| Rubin volume ramp by fiscal Q4 FY2027 (NVIDIA) | 3 | 3 | all both hold | |\n| Groq 3 LPX volume shipments in fiscal Q3 FY2027 (NVIDIA) | 2 | 2 | all both hold | |\n| Hugging Face acquisition closing by 2028-03-24 (NVIDIA or Hugging Face) | 1 | 2 | both fail, twice (settled, no third reader) | \"will not close by the date … a forecast of delay … the deal still pending\" |\n| Taiwan's export controls by 2027-01-31 (an authority) | 1 | 1 | split; both third readers fail | the wait (D420) |\n| **Total** | **34** (company: 33 over 6 matters) | **45** (company: 44) | | |\n\n## The whole rung, read as the judge and the page see it\n\nIn every answer, rung 1 states the event **with its date**. Examples:\n- \"NVIDIA begins shipping Vera Rubin by December 31, 2026 …\";\n- \"NVIDIA reports Vera Rubin volume customer shipments with first revenue recognition on or before the end of FY2027\";\n- \"NVIDIA completes the Hugging Face acquisition by March 24, 2028\".\n\nThe Ruled-out rung is the model's decision followed by code's sentence: \"This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the\nevent of rung 1 as a whole, … was made after <article day> and stands on <due day>, and only when rung 1 does not.\"\n\n- **A company's formal announcement that the event will not happen by the due day** is a decision against rung 1's event as written:\n  the dated event, as a whole. The reader brief's R row says the same: \"the `Yes` condition is the event the question asks about; the\n  first sentence of the `Ruled out` condition … is a decision against that event as a whole … and not the event's merely being still\n  awaited\".\n  - **Qualified in round 149:** an announcement is not necessarily a decision. \"It will not happen by the date\" can be a forecast of\n    delay rather than the company deciding against the dated event. Some dissenting reasons draw exactly that line (\"can be a\n    forecast of delay\"), so it is too strong to say they all ignored the date. Any future reading keeps a forecast-versus-decision\n    guard. The frozen step's sentences mostly name a decision (\"formally announces\", \"formally decides to defer\", \"postponed\"); some\n    say only \"announces that … will not …\", which is where that guard bites.\n- **A delay inside the deadline** cannot rule out: code's \"only when rung 1 does not\" and \"stands on the due day\" leave rung 1 to\n  hold if the event happens by the due day.\n- **A changed decision** that no longer stands on the due day does not rule out, by code's sentence.\n- **A cancellation** rules out in every answer read.\n- **An authority's proceeding** follows D420 and the brief's own clause: its formal abandonment or withdrawal rules it out, and its\n  going on is the wait.\n  - Taiwan p3's \"will not adopt … by January 31, 2027\" names a timetable while the proceeding goes on. That is the wait, so it is a\n    misapplication.\n  - It is one send of three; the topic's other two name abandonment or withdrawal.\n  - The model's own account is on record (`taiwan-account-output.txt`): it carried the deadline into the decision.\n\n## What it gives\n\n- **No general model error is shown in the company-delay class.** Across 6 company matters (33 distinct answers), the frozen step's\n  ruling outs read as decisions agree with rung 1's dated event, code's sentence, D409, D416 and the reader brief. The open edge is\n  forecast against decision, not a missing rule.\n- **D606's provisional reading misstates these questions.** That reading was \"a formal postponement where the event remains possible\n  reads Still open\". Read against the undated event, it would leave a dated question \"Still open\" after its date while the company\n  has already announced it will not happen by then. **By D606's own reversal condition** (\"if worked cases show it misstates the\n  question an analyst holds\"), the reading is reversed:\n  - for an event with a due day, the event is rung 1's, with its date;\n  - a decision that it will not happen by the due day, standing on the due day, rules it out;\n  - an authority's proceeding follows D420.\n- **The authority kind is one isolated misapplication** (Taiwan p3, one send of three).\n- **By D606's criterion the route closes,** since it found only isolated misapplications and no general rule is missing. Line 3 stays\n  unmet on the fresh week by that one send.\n- **Found on the way, another class, not pursued here:** two Kyber answers whose Yes (\"by 2027-12-31\") is wider than their question\n  (\"during 2027\"). That is a question-to-rung window mismatch (the Q and R marks), 2 answers on 1 matter.\n- **My error:** in round 148 I proposed \"Still open\" as my lean. My recorded lean on Alex's page (D591) was the opposite, \"rules out\",\n  and I did not say I had changed it. The audit supports the D591 lean.\n# The shared fiscal-year note, audited (D607's step), 2026-11-16, $0\n\n**Who reads the note** (`universe.fiscal_note`):\n\n- production extraction's request (`src/hivemind/extraction.py:387`, frozen);\n- the answer-choice step's payload (`clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/stage_a/stage_a.py:144`, frozen as the ladder step, D426);\n- the gate-1 readers' packets (`stage_a/build_reader_packets.py:35`).\n\nFor NVDA the note ends: \"FY2027's full-year figures are published in the annual report around 2027-02; use that day as the due\ndate\". The shared function is not edited.\n\n**What the clause could reach in the answer-choice step:**\n\n- **The shown day (`ladder_step.due_day_of`):** code replaces the model's day only when the model chose the basis\n  `expected_publication` (D414: year-end plus 60 days for a company figure). A `stated` day is kept. So the clause could reach the\n  product only through the model's choice of basis, or through the `stated` days it writes.\n- **`basis_census.py`** → `basis-census-output.txt`: 202 distinct company-question answers from the frozen step (probe readings'\n  control sends only):\n\n  | Basis | The day falls in the note's month | Another day |\n  |---|---|---|\n  | `expected_publication` (code sets D414's day) | 23 | 102 |\n  | `stated` (code keeps the answer's day) | 3 | 74 |\n\n  - The 23 `expected_publication` answers in the note's month are model days that code replaces.\n  - The 74 `stated` answers on another day are, as read, events and amounts with a deadline the question itself names: shipments by\n    2026-12-31, the Hugging Face deal by 2028-03-24, a guarantee by 2027-12-31.\n  - The 3 `stated` answers in the note's month are one matter: \"Rubin volume ramp by January 31, 2027, shown by the fiscal Q4\n    earnings release\", dated 2027-02-24 or 2027-02-28, the release that settles it. That is defensible as stated, and not shown to\n    come from the note.\n- **Year:** the year mapping (\"this year is FY2027 …\") is in the note's other clauses, which no change proposed here would touch.\n- **Refusal and rung:** nothing in the stored answers links either to the due-day clause.\n- **The check before code's replacement (round 150):** for `expected_publication`, code first refuses a written day before the\n  year's end (`ladder_step.py` check 5). The note's \"around 2027-02\" gives the model a day after the year-end. Removing the clause\n  could therefore raise code refusals, so the clause is not inert. A change would need that measured.\n- **The D split** (D590: Codex readers fail D on code-set days): the readers' packet shows the same note beside D414's day.\n  - Removing the clause from the step's request would not change the packet.\n  - Changing the packet is a change of the reading form, which D590 decided against.\n  - **The cause of the split is not established:** the D-only diagnostic did not reproduce the split, so the packet's note is a\n    candidate cause, not a shown one.\n\n**Result by D607's criterion:**\n\n- No product-visible effect is shown: the shown day is D414's or the question's own deadline.\n- The reader split's cause is not established, so no gate-relevant effect of the request is shown.\n- **Closed as a cleanup-only lead.** The step's request carries a due-day instruction that code overrides (AGENTS §3 and §6). No new\n  version and no paid send follow.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,100p' docs/CURRENT_STATE.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Current state\n\n**Clean Steps 1–3, current (2026-11-11).** Records: Step 1, [round log](../clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md) §§16–47; Step 2, [Step 2 log](../clean-arch/step2-validation-2026-11-07/LOG.md) §§12–19.\n- **Step 1 configuration: c8, adopted by Alex (D387).** It runs DeepSeek Flash at high effort, with a 64,000-token output cap; a\n  format failure or a cut answer is resent once (D380). It carries Alex's decisions D382–D385:\n  - figures are conditions;\n  - material statements with no thesis are kept without links;\n  - only big matters open a thesis;\n  - no target prices.\n\n  [PRD §12](../clean-arch/PRD.md) holds its system prompt, closing check and schema; a run with no candidate files sends exactly the\n  accepted request.\n- **Acceptance (D386, [protocol](../clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/ACCEPTANCE-PROTOCOL.md)).** 50 fresh articles that no run,\n  old or new, had used: news from 2026-09-05 to 09-07, a holiday weekend with no NVIDIA posts. One draw; two readers and a third on\n  disputes.\n  - helpful 132/139 (core 83/83);\n  - wrongly opened theses 1 of 10;\n  - search 141/143 available repeat pairs, or 141/145 for the whole system (one 11-article story supplies 55 pairs, and revisions are\n    barely exercised);\n  - clear wrong merges 1/57, plus 2 overlaps;\n  - detail retention 22 = 22. This is a storage comparison: every record keeps its own text. It is not a usability test.\n\n  Dev ×3 on the frozen golden-v5 (a lead): helpful 214, 211 and 212 of 234; search 98.9–100%; clear wrong merges about 1%. Step 1\n  cost for the fresh set plus dev ×3: $4.20 at published rates.\n- **Steps 2 and 3** are built in isolation (`clean-arch/step2`, `clean-arch/step3`): search, comparison, grouping and later linking.\n  They are not integrated with production storage. **Step 4 (judging) has not passed** on the clean path: its ladder step is built and\n  measured (four Stage A runs, lines missed by 1 to 3 answers; D426), so nothing yet shows the clean system can judge an investment\n  view on its own questions.\n- **Step 2 is closed and frozen (Alex, D427, 2026-11-11;** [Step 2 log](../clean-arch/step2-validation-2026-11-07/LOG.md) §§14–19**).** It goes\n  to production only with the whole clean path, after Step 4 meets its lines; `tests/test_frozen_clean_step2.py` guards its files. Its three lines were met at the acceptance above (one draw; \"later version\"\n  relations barely exercised), and no miss of the seeded end-to-end test (clean 18, 18, 17 against production 17, 15, 16) has\n  been traced to it (the two single-draw misses were diagnosed on 2026-11-11, below: neither traces to Step 2). What happened since the close-out of 2026-11-08:\n  - A change to Jev's wording for \"later version\" relations failed its line (42 calls, 2026-11-09) and tuning stopped.\n  - One wrong relation was traced to the judge: it put NVIDIA's answer to the Kyber report under the FY2027 revenue question;\n    sent both ways, the conclusion did not differ ([result](../clean-arch/step2-edge-effect-2026-11-10/RESULT.md)).\n  - Alex stopped work on that relation (D423) and asked for a fix to the rule that gives a \"later version\" the older record's\n    questions. Measured on the three saved draws ([round log](../clean-arch/step2-revision-links-2026-11-10/LOG.md)), removing the\n    rule was withdrawn: of the 24 links it wrote, two blind reader families call 19 context and 3 evidence, and one of the 3 is a\n    company's answer that would otherwise be missing beside four reports (with it rung 2, 2, 2; without it 2, 3, 2). **The rule\n    stays (D424).**\n  - **Open, recorded:** Jev's \"later version\" relation (9 of 30 join different statements in draw 1); the context links it carries\n    (19 of 24); wrong groups (at the line on one draw; the unprompted reading test never run); the \"Unverified quote\" mark not\n    shown to Jev (3 of 188); not decided, whether a company's answer must sit under every question its report is filed under.\n  - **Reopens when** Step 4 yields judged views on the clean path and a miss, or something a reader of a thesis sees, traces to\n    one of these. The round's measurement kit (replay of a saved draw, exact simulation of a writer rule, blind packets with\n    controls, a judgement sent both ways) is kept in the two folders above.\n- **The clean system's untraced end-to-end misses, diagnosed (2026-11-11;** [round log](../clean-arch/e2e-misses-2026-11-11/LOG.md)**).**\n  The China share question in draw 3 and the FY2028 revenue question in draw 2 (seeded test, D392). Both estimates sit on a rung\n  line. China: the same window-end request, re-sent, gives the miss twice and the right answer once (the judge's own variation at the\n  1% line); no H200 price is in any request. FY2028: draw 2's window-end request gives the miss 3 of 3 and draw 3's the right answer 3\n  of 3; draw 2's lacks records draw 3's holds (a \"41% in calendar 2027\" figure Step 1 filed under FY2027 only, \"$1 trillion of\n  Blackwell and Rubin visibility\", five infrastructure-demand records), not isolated which matters; FY2028 is the question whose filed\n  statements differ most between draws (47% shared, median 74%). Neither traces to Step 2. Refuted on the way: that a refused answer\n  re-asked caused the China miss. Observed defect of the frozen judge: it names the export-controls theme as a dependency of the China\n  question, 42 of 65 refusals in six draws, and the re-ask changed the rung 8 times (PRD 11, E4). No change proposed; 18 sends, $0.043.\n- **Open defects, by product impact** (reflection council, `council/av-codex.md`):\n  1. **The Ohio-guarantee yes/no question is never covered** in the three dev draws; a broader credit-support amount thesis opens\n     instead. Diagnosed (round log §48): the model folds the unsigned guarantee into the open aggregate thesis because the request\n     uses \"covers\" both for \"is evidence for\" and for \"already asks this question\". The same clause sits under link clutter.\n  2. **A revenue-share thesis was opened** (Vera Rubin's share of data-center revenue), the class-A boundary of D382.\n  3. **Link clutter.** A blind recheck of the 34 disputed fresh links found 19 weak and 2 wrong; 10 accepted links mixed in as controls were all useful; the other 49 links were not rechecked.\n     The model's own account names topic or theme overlap and inference chains, and c8's closing check never reads back a link. This is\n     a likely cause, with no fix proven. Alex approved designing a protected link check through the council; a first draft was stopped\n     because it would drop founding premises and contrary facts.\n  4. **Unverified passages:** 3 of 188 fresh records and 1–3 per dev draw. The mark now travels into Step 2's records, and a reader sees the found source text or an \"Unverified quote\" label. Jev's comparison input still carries the model's text without the mark; changing it changes measured Step 2 behaviour and needs its own measurement.\n  5. **The lidar policy thesis** recurs at the boundary (one dev draw in three).\n- **History.** These earlier states are superseded:\n  - the 2026-11-07 takeover (K5 → K6b; [takeover log](../clean-arch/takeover-2026-11-07/LOG.md));\n  - the 2026-11-08 close-out (K6b test 32/42; Step 2 first lines; [Step 2 log](../clean-arch/step2-validation-2026-11-07/LOG.md) §12);\n  - the unfiled fix round and the c1–c7 arms (round log §§1–39).\n- **No run is live.** The branch is committed through the D388 line (round log §§48–59).\n\n**Clean Step 1 source investigation (2026-11-07):** A [source-loss census](../clean-arch/STEP1-SOURCE-LOSS-CENSUS.md) traces two separate losses in the historical cleaner and records reproducible corpus observations. Offline candidate comparisons expose both recovered content and source-identity or contamination risks. A subsequent [full-page diagnostic](../clean-arch/STEP1-FULL-PAGE-RESULTS.md) completed 102 calls on 17 selected articles and independent source-backed review. It exposes examples of both missing or misattributed source text and model errors that persist despite the right text being present. A Codex–Claude council is reviewing the results and earlier attempts; no source-cleaning candidate is adopted, and the source corpus and production defaults are unchanged.\n\nAlex has authorized correcting the test-source cleaner and test data when their defects are confirmed, while the frozen production configuration and ruler remain intact. The cleaner switched 615 originally fed articles off; a replay reproduces all 615, and direct inspection confirms an actual article excluded because its 9,874-character body was flattened to one line. Other examples include a wrong-site shell, paywall and long challenge code, so the false-exclusion count and a general article-identification repair remain to be established. The [council regroup](../clean-arch/STEP1-SOURCE-REGROUP-COUNCIL.md) and [source-preservation design](../clean-arch/STEP1-SOURCE-PRESERVATION-DESIGN.md) describe an isolated candidate checked against all 34,084 records, with a fake-answer actual-runner handoff and provenance display in the replay renderer. The [five source-control calls](../clean-arch/STEP1-CAPTURE-CONTROLS-RESULTS.md) completed and were source-reviewed; two items contain summary details absent from their attached quotations, and one source-visible risk explanation was not returned. This is selected-case evidence, not a general quality result. Broader source-quality comparison, citation completeness and actual writer integration remain open.\n\n**Clean architecture Step 1 (2026-11-06, isolated work on `clean-arch`):** Alex reopened work with the clean architecture. An experimental prepared-source handoff now preserves the archived page, prior cleaned text, source-role decisions and selected-line mapping without changing the original database; quotations for prepared items must also occur contiguously in the original captured page. Two seven-item runs are available in the [clean architecture PRD](../clean-arch/PRD.md), one in the earlier selection order and one in recorded page-timestamp order. The source selector is not a production service and source ordering is not established event-arrival ordering. The runs expose unresolved semantic problems: citations can omit supporting facts despite an exact location match; model output can change a benchmark into a deployment, reverse a backup relation, omit a named customer or assign an unstated currency. A blinded 84-call check-placement/wording comparison and a 27-call same-article citation-completion diagnostic did not give a clean semantic fix and are not adopted. The latter missed an omitted connectivity condition and a reversed backup relationship, and left six expected evidence reviews unaddressed. A further five-round Codex–Claude council produced a narrowly scoped Step 1 link candidate, independently checked for prompt-contract drift. A six-article, 36-call blind comparison completed; the candidate still produced an explicitly self-contradictory link and both arms consistently omitted the same Kyber report and company response. It is not adopted and has not changed the active PRD prompt. A trace confirms that both the Kyber report and open Kyber thesis were supplied, and the raw model answers omitted the report and denial; the reason is not established. See ../poc/data/clean-step1-kyber-trace-2026-11-06/TRACE.md. An isolated six-call comparison showed that hiding the Kyber thesis did not recover the report or denial in any draw. A second six-call comparison returned them in 3/3 draws with the original Kyber paragraph alone and 0/3 with the full article; length, position and surrounding content changed together, so the reason is not isolated. Excerpt outputs have source-attribution limitations. Neither experiment changed state or established a production fix. (Superseded: the next task then was a whole-article coverage approach; the current next task is under \"One next task\".) See ../clean-arch/STEP1-IMPLEMENTATION-COUNCIL.md. The earlier four-round council agreed that no tested semantic fix is ready; its next investigation is a source-backed review of the 49-item historical run and its 160 evidence records, with overlapping issue flags and separate article-level omissions, then a separate comparison of the seven-item replays. See ../clean-arch/STEP1-COUNCIL-REVIEW.md. Preserve relevant evidence while identifying the next intervention; production/frozen history remains as described below. See [Step 1 run log](../clean-arch/STEP1-RUN-LOG.md) and [English reading page](../clean-arch/architecture.html).\n\n**Sealed (D379, 2026-11-06):** Alex asked to clean up and seal this version while he re-plans the system design. Work stops here and is sealed as it is: main's working tree is committed on branch `seal/2026-11-06` (tag `sealed-2026-11-06`) and merged into `main` on Alex's word (PR #10); the sealed split and two side experiments are pushed on their own branches (D379). No PRD 11 experiment is open, and the E1 next task below is withdrawn. The next task is Alex's re-planned system design.\n\n**E1 diagnosed (2026-11-06, Alex chose E1):** of 57 helpful matters frozen production wrote and then dropped, two readers agree on 17 link misses (4 core), 3 opening misses (1 core) and 24 small matters the standard itself drops; 10 are disputed on the fallback clause and 3 were quote refusals. Opening is not the experiment. The core of the link misses is the model reading a question's period, population or named configuration as a boundary; only 8 of the 13 window draws it dropped were lost to the product. No production change; $0.02 of probes. My recommendation was to fold that narrow change into the next extraction version; withdrawn by the seal (D379). Details: `docs/research/2026-11-06-e1-no-home-drops/README.md`, PRD 11 E1.\n\n**Split extraction sealed as a failed experiment (D378, 2026-11-06):** the split (a new-question call, an evidence call and record preparation) was benchmarked against the frozen poc-v108 and failed. Its stop line fired: helpful matters 156, 156, 159 against 166, 170, 162, with about 15% higher cost. One screened revision kept more noise than production. It is sealed in the worktree `hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review` (tag `split-extraction-failed-sealed-2026-11-06`), and nothing of it is in main. What it taught is written as proposed production experiments in `docs/prd/11-production-experiments-from-split.md`: E1 no-home helpful drops, E2 duplicate questions, E3 ladder checks with D186, E4 judge refusals, E5 D203's revision day, E6 the measurement kit. Each opens only on Alex's word.\n\n**Question-catalog limit removed (D377, 2026-11-05):** Alex also directed removal of the separate 150 KB catalog rejection. The research reader in this worktree now has neither the catalog nor evidence byte limit; it retains full scoped questions, historical boundaries, dependencies and evidence. A portable 326,996-byte catalog test returns all 400 new questions and expands evidence both after reading the catalog and directly; the old 150 KB guard fails the same test in a scratch copy. The saved 69-question pilot catalog and all 69 saved evidence expansions match their original content hashes. Details: `docs/research/2026-11-05-catalog-limit/`. The full suite remains pending until the paid seven-day run no longer competes for resources. The run remains in its isolated worktree and does not call this research reader.\n\n**Evidence reader limit removed (D376, 2026-11-05):** Alex directed removal of the reader's 1 MB evidence-expansion rejection. In this worktree, `research_context.Reader.expand` now returns the entire evidence payload without that cap, while the catalog's separate limit remained at that checkpoint; D377 subsequently removed it. On the saved pilot ledger, all 69 individual question expansions, the four-question 1,931,225-byte pilot request and the entire 3,454,076-byte catalog produce the exact same content as the pre-change high-limit baseline; the portable oversized-ledger test passes and fails when the old cap is restored in a scratch copy. Details and snapshot-specific measurements are in `docs/research/2026-11-05-reader-limit/`. The seven-day diagnostic runs in `/Users/lchingen/hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review/poc/data/split-fresh-seven-day-2026-11-05-v2/` with its pinned code; it does not call this research reader. Read its status through its `snapshot.sh`; do not edit its package code while it is live. This change is not an acceptance of seven-day extraction or judgement quality.\n\n**Start-state correction (2026-11-05):** Alex requires a research library established from real material available before the test start, then extraction against that state. The prior empty-library diagnostic cannot represent this case. Our own mistakes and the current 68-question provenance audit are in `../hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review/docs/research/2026-11-05-parallel-refinement/own-mistakes-2026-11-05.zh.md`. Two new A-only calls were run on 2026-08-29 articles using the 68-question prior-day snapshot; both proposed the same aggregate supply-commitments topic. A source/context review judged it distinct from the existing specific SK Hynix question. A third A-only call on an author-body excerpt about DLSS 5, already tracked by v22, passed; the prompt also restricts software updates, so the reason is not established. No writer or dedup ran; source-history completeness remains unverified, and this is not scored acceptance. Details: ../hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review/docs/research/2026-11-05-parallel-refinement/start-state-diagnostic-2026-11-05.zh.md.\n\n**Measurement correction (2026-11-05):** Alex clarified that a gold-worthy topic already in the supplied library should not open again; an absent worthy topic should open. The earlier simple-A claim of a \"missed\" new topic was not scored against that rule and is withdrawn as a correctness conclusion. In the actual experiment, a Kyber topic was shown and passing follows Alex's topic-level rule; whether its exact release question captures the shipping milestone remains unverified. The experimental pre-article snapshots differ from the replay's day-start snapshots; 9 of 10 inspected examples differ. The current uncommitted replay default does not build a cold-start library; committed main HEAD still uses the old seed and startup filing path. Historical lexical coverage has a confirmed unrelated-topic match. The frozen gold and past scores remain unchanged. Audit: `../hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review/docs/research/2026-11-05-parallel-refinement/measurement-context-scrub-2026-11-05.zh.md`.\n\n**Simple new-question diagnostic (2026-11-05):** At Alex's direction, an isolated prompt returned new questions plus article evidence without asking for product database fields. Four previously seen real dev articles each ran once: three returned one candidate, one returned none, all four quotes matched their source, and the gold-listed GPU backstop amount question was not proposed. Whether that omission is a new-topic miss depends on whether the existing library covers the topic. Two other candidates require source and independent-significance review; the actual experimental snapshot already had a Kyber topic, so its pass follows Alex's topic-level rule; whether its exact release question captures the shipping milestone remains unverified. This is not a production or full-pipeline acceptance. Details: `../hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review/docs/research/2026-11-05-parallel-refinement/simple-new-question-results.zh.md`.\n\n**Split prompt structure v3 read diagnostic (2026-11-05):** One real reading-stage call on a previously seen article found the revenue/margin guidance and Trainium scale but omitted the gold China revenue/market-loss matter. The prewritten stop condition was triggered and the linking call was not sent. This is one draw and not a production or end-to-end result. See `../hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review/docs/research/2026-11-05-parallel-refinement/structure-v3-read-result.zh.md`. D374 removes the model sampling field from active settings and default configs; historical config files and archives retain their original bytes.\n\n**Split prompt structure v2 small test (2026-11-05):** Thirty calls on two previously seen golden-v5 dev articles completed in `../hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review`. The blind model reader found complete helpful matters in the fresh control versus new A+B of 6/19 vs 10/19, 11/19 vs 7/19, and 9/19 vs 12/19. The new arm also retained more gold not-kept matters in each draw; B missed useful margin guidance and one answer reused IDs, with a demonstrated matching collision. This is not a production acceptance. See `../hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review/docs/research/2026-11-05-parallel-refinement/structure-v2-results.zh.md` and `reports/2026-11-05-parallel-structure-v2.html`.\n\n**Split extraction small test (2026-11-05):** At Alex's request, the isolated `hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review` worktree ran two real golden-v5 articles once through each split lane. An independent model reader found 7 of 19 helpful gold matters fully retained, two partial, two written then dropped, eight missed, and one retained gold not-kept matter. Three saved v108 draws on these articles retained 7, 9 and 12 of 19, but used 22 seeded questions while the new test used 8/10-question snapshots; these are not directly comparable. The experiment remains unaccepted; see `../hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review/docs/research/2026-11-05-parallel-refinement/small-scored-test-20261105.md` and `reports/2026-11-05-split-small-test.html`.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,138p;331,416p' clean-arch/PRD.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Hivemind architecture: a market world model built the way a hedge-fund analyst works\n\n**Status, 2026-11-16: working design of the rebuilt pipeline (\"the new system\", this folder).** Step 1 runs the adopted request c8\n(D387). Step 2 and Step 3's grouping are frozen (D427). Step 4's ladder step is built and frozen as it ran (D426); production's frozen\njudge follows it. The new system has not yet passed the gates that would let it replace the running pipeline (section 8). Until it\ndoes, the running pipeline (`poc/config/poc-v118.json`) stays the default and both are kept (D544). Six choices wait on Alex\n([decision page](../docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/DECISION-PAGE-2026-11-16.md)). Nothing here is deployed.\n\n**How to read this document.** Section 1 says what the system is for, the problems it answers and what it does differently. Section 2\nis the picture. Sections 3 to 7 define the information and each step. Section 8 says how each step is measured and where it stands.\nSections 9 to 11 walk one item through, list what is open and draw the boundary. Sections 12 and 13 hold the two frozen requests word\nfor word. Sections 14 to 16 map the code, record the future design for links across companies and point to the earlier record.\n\n## 1. Vision, problems and what is done differently\n\n### 1.1 The vision: a market world model\n\nAlex set the purpose when this architecture started: \"the purpose of this system is to build a hedge fund brain, it is building the\ndecison process for hedgefund analyst to source data and extract information (thesis) and link evidences for keeping it's dossier of\nevents that impact the assets and events in the world\" (2026-11-06).\n\nHe now calls it a market world model (2026-11-16). In this document the phrase means one concrete thing: **a standing, dated record of\nthe open investment questions about each company and theme, which only new information moves and which closes each\nquestion on its due day.** For each question\nthe record holds:\n\n- **The question, fixed when it opened**, with its possible answers and its due day written before any evidence is judged. Alex:\n  \"一条观点一定是有一个明确的 ladder 和到期日，这样才构成一个观点呀\" (D38).\n- **Every statement that bears on it**: who said it, when, where it was reported, and every later report or revision of it.\n- **The current answer and its history.** The answer moves only when new information arrives. Alex: \"有相关的信息抽取出现，自然会影响整个系统抽取的thesis 向前\" (D13).\n- **The final answer on the due day.** Alex: \"最后到期日的状态就定格就好\" (D54).\n\nThe whole system builds, updates and retires that record. Alex: \"其实整个system做的事情就是建构，更新，删除这个data structure\" (D26).\n\n**One picture.** On NVIDIA, a report says it may back OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio. The record opens one question:\n\"Will NVIDIA sign a guarantee or backstop for OpenAI's Ohio data-center lease by 2027-12-31?\" Its answers are fixed at opening: it\nhappened, it is ruled out, or still open; due 2027-12-31. The amount of the guarantee is listed as a part not judged. Every later\nreport of the same story joins one group instead of counting again. A company denial is filed as a later version of the report. The\nanswer moves only on a statement that changes it. On the due day the answer is closed and kept.\n\n**Why a reader uses it.** Alex: \"it's like a dossier, right? Or it's like a reference where people can anchor their beliefs, or they\ncan check whatever they're thinking, whether it's already priced in, or, you know, how single factors evolve. I don't think the value\nis in, like, having an idea and basically just constructing alpha\" (2026-10-22). The gap it closes: \"大部分的人, 每一天都会有很多信息,\n但这些信息怎么变成交易? … 你要拆解这个信息, 理解它背后的驱动力啊, 哪些因子会影响他 … 这个才是真正中间转移的痛点\" (2026-10-22). On a\npage: \"一个用户的thesis 可以直接被拆解成可以理解的证据，并且看得到证据过去变化的历史\" (2026-10-24).\n\n**What it is not.**\n- Not a tracker for a view the user already holds. Alex rejected that framing: \"我只是把这个东西当成一个跟踪工具, 我就是完全是不对的呀\"\n  (2026-10-22). The system forms the questions itself, before any user asks.\n- Not a price forecast or an alpha engine (his words above).\n- Not yet a judge of what is priced in. The record keeps the expectations it reads (a consensus estimate, a company's guidance) as\n  evidence, and a figure's ranges may be set against a consensus (section 7). But no step compares a judged answer with what the\n  market already prices, so the record cannot by itself say whether an answer is in the price (section 10).\n\n### 1.2 The problems it answers\n\nThree things make an analyst's job hard, and the record is built against each:\n\n1. **Most of what arrives is noise; the increment is rare.** Alex: \"不是什么事情都是惊弓之鸟需要抽取，杂音还是很大的，我们整体应该倾向谨慎…同时我们要敏感一些新的信息带来的增量不要错过\" (D243).\n   The system keeps few statements and only big questions. Alex: \"我觉得核心就是这个颗粒度的把控要符合一个高端的hedge fund analyst 的思维。不要搜集一堆鸡毛蒜皮的事情，要看重要的大事，合并整合关键的信息\" (D248).\n2. **One statement arrives many times.** The same forecast comes through many outlets and reposts. Counted as many, it fakes\n   conviction. The record keeps it as one statement with all its sources (section 6).\n3. **An answer written from scratch has no memory and no score.** It cannot say what it concluded two weeks ago, what changed it, or\n   whether it was right. The record fixes the question and its answers first, dates every judgement with its evidence, and closes the\n   answer on the due day.\n\n**Why one model call over everything does not do this.** Alex, on asking a general model to research theses: \"it's actually very, very\nhard because it hallucinates, it, you know, it doesn't really work\" (2026-10-22). Two measurements point at what breaks:\n- **Too much material in one request fails.** In a three-month run the data-center revenue question held 1,768 linked pieces. Its\n  judging request was 292 KB with 698 evidence ids. The judge mislabelled ids, its answers were refused, and whole days halted (D358;\n  Alex: \"bloating evidence 的问题 … 很严重，需要解决\").\n- **The reasoning is not the bottleneck; delivering the right facts to the right question is.** The custom-chip question, sent as it\n  was, answered \"below $40 billion\" 9 times of 9. With the Broadcom and Trainium facts the ledger already held added, it left the\n  lowest range 9 times of 9 and reasoned as the expert readers did (`docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md`, Evidence).\n\nSo the architecture puts its effort into reading, filing and grouping, and keeps the judge small and fixed. No test has compared the\nsystem with a single general-model call on the same questions; the case rests on the measurements above.\n\n### 1.3 Why the running pipeline was rebuilt\n\nOn 2026-11-06 Alex sealed the running pipeline's line of work and restarted the design. \"好的我觉得你先clean up封存这个版本吧。我要重新梳理系统设计\" (D379).\n\"之前所有的rules decisions 你可以参考，但是我们要重新开始做一个干净的\". \"之前很多的carry over 是不正确的 … 我觉的我们first principle 重来\".\nHe named the shape that does not scale: \"thesis counts are okay after it grows, like <100, but evidence attached grows, so if feeding\ninto the extraction it's too much context\". And he drew the new one: \"{thesis, evidences} extraction in pairs, with what existing thesis\nthat is outstanding as input context … then in the nex stage I can just dedup this evidence without having the need to read the full\nevidence\"; \"there should be like a search engine overlay on top of evidence db for a {thesis, evidence} to return the top-10\nsemantically relevant evidence\" (all 2026-11-06; his [diagram](reference-diagram.png)).\n\nThe running pipeline's measured problems:\n\n| Problem | What was measured | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| Evidence piles up under a few questions | 1,768 pieces under one question; a 292 KB judging request; refused answers halted days. | D358 |\n| A fact with no question is thrown away | 441 statements dropped as \"no home\" over three draws; 60 of 213 missed helpful matters were written, then dropped. In one fresh week, 278 thrown away. | `docs/prd/11-production-experiments-from-split.md` E1; `docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/week/WEEK-RESULT.md` |\n| A fallback parks what filing cannot place | Under the total-revenue question, 32–39% of its reports are ones the standard does not keep and only 7–10% are what the gold uses; 24–44% of its judgement citations are about other companies or the whole market. | `docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md` |\n| Narrow questions starve | The custom-chip question received none of Broadcom's figures in three draws; operating margin and NVIDIA's capital spending were never judged. | `docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md` |\n| Questions crowd into financial lines | Alex: \"永远都是一堆财务指标，真正的观点呢？\" | D164 |\n| The same bet opens twice | One bet split on a fine detail; ten re-sends of one stored request opened a duplicate twice. | D354 |\n| Earlier versions are searched by shared words inside one question | 82.1% of known earlier versions found; a newly opened question gets no history. | [Step 2 log](step2-validation-2026-11-07/LOG.md) |\n| Answer choices written by extraction can be inverted or empty | A favourable rung holding the unfavourable range; a band missing its reference; a rung with no value. | `docs/prd/11-production-experiments-from-split.md` E3 |\n\n### 1.4 What the new system does differently\n\n| Choice | Running pipeline | New system | Shown so far |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| What reading an item sees | Open questions with filing guidance, from the start of the day. | Every open thesis as the previous item left it, and never the evidence history (section 4). | The request no longer grows with the evidence. |\n| What opens a question | Extraction proposes candidates with answer choices; a matcher and a collision judge decide. | Step 1 decides in the same reading, and only for a big matter; a company's own figures are conditions, not questions (D382, D384). | 1 of 10 opened wrongly on 50 fresh articles (D386). Opening is also where it loses most against the ruler (section 8). |\n| A useful fact no question takes | Thrown away. | Kept without links, and offered to each new question when it opens (D383). | 0 thrown away against 278 in one fresh week; but 389 of 569 kept statements were read by no judgement that week (section 10). |\n| Finding the earlier version of a statement | Shared words, inside one question. | Semantic search across the company, then Jev compares (section 5). | 141 of 143 earlier versions found; 1 clear wrong merge in 57 (D386). |\n| The same statement reported again | A separate record. | One group; each report keeps its own text and source (section 6). | Later details are kept (22 = 22, a storage check). Copies are still 31–34% of what the judge reads, against 26–31% in the running pipeline. |\n| Answer choices | Written by extraction with the candidate. | A separate step at opening: an event has three outcomes; a figure has five ranges against a reference with the favourable end registered; the due day follows rules (section 7). | Its quality gate is not met (section 8). |\n| Judging and closing | The frozen judge, daily; closing on the due day. | The same judge and the same closing, unchanged (D354, D567). | By design, differences come only from what reaches the judge. |\n\nOn the frozen extraction gold, Step 1 keeps more of what matters: helpful 214, 211 and 212 of 234 in three draws, against 167, 171 and\n162 for the running extraction (core 85–86 of 86 against 75–76). The running pipeline's draws were stored runs from another day, so\nthis is a lead, not a same-day comparison.\n\n**What is not shown yet.** That the new system gives better answers end to end. Every comparison so far is a diagnostic: the seeded\ntest was inconclusive (18, 18, 17 against 17, 15, 16 of 23); on one fresh week the difference was \"not clear\" by the rule written\nbeforehand (D489). The weakest point is named (D559): the system often cannot turn an important matter into a question it can answer\nand follow. It also costs more: about $3 a draw for the whole new system (Step 1 about $2.5 of it) against about $0.45 for the running\npipeline, and about 2 hours 20 minutes a draw because items are read one after another (an estimate,\n`e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/SLICE-RESULT.md`). Correct comes\nbefore cheap; cost is recorded as a baseline.\n\n### 1.5 The gates before it replaces the running pipeline\n\nThe new system replaces the running pipeline only after it passes five gates, in order (D548, written by the council under D439;\nD544):\n\n| Gate | Its lines, and whose | Where it stands |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1. The ladder step (Stage A) | Alex's six lines (D400): no question other than the topic's; no part left unlisted; no outcome with no range or with two; no unjustified decline; 35 of 44 topics right in two of three sends, 28 of them with written answer choices; the same favourable end in all three sends and the same due day for nine in ten. | Not passed; missed in all nine readings. |\n| 2. The judge on the system's own questions (Stage B) | D428: unsupported figure answers at most five points above the baseline; unsupported yes/no answers at most 5%; unsupported last answers at most 3 in 30; enough controls. | Not passed: 4 comparable controls against 8 needed; the strictest last-answer reading missed (3 of 24). |\n| 3. End to end against the running pipeline | D427, D431: both systems, unseeded, the same day, three draws each, on the frozen ruler, with Step 2's three lines checked in the same run. | Owed. \"Beats\" is not yet defined in numbers. |\n| 4. Use on unseen news | Opened by Alex beside the others (D484). Its lines (four in five big matters covered; at most one in five questions trivial or duplicate; nine in ten last answers supported; at most one in twenty cited statements wrong; no undelivered fact that would change an answer) are proposed, not his. | Not an accepted gate yet. |\n| 5. Integration and release | The whole path on production storage and the product's pages, accepted on the product's own path. | Partly built: the product page and API read a store of the new system (D570–D573); the live-store contract is agreed for test stores only (D593). |\n\n## 7. Step 4: judge the thesis\n\nJudgement is separate from reading and saving an item. Saving the revised capital-spending forecast records **what the company said**;\njudgement says **what that means for the question**, from all the saved evidence. Alex opened this step's design on 2026-11-10:\n\"设计新系统自己的“判断”环节\" (D396). The full design is [Step 4 design](step4-2026-11-10/DESIGN.md) (version 35); this section states\nwhat it does.\n\n### Two parts\n\n1. **The ladder step (Step 4a).** One call per newly opened thesis, after Step 3 has saved the item that opened it and before that\n   day's judging. It reads the thesis, the article that opened it, the company and its fiscal-year note, and the list of themes; it\n   reads nothing later than the opening article. It writes the judged question, its answer choices and its due day, and lists every\n   other part of the thesis as not judged. Model: gpt-6-luna at medium effort.\n2. **The judge (Step 4b).** Production's frozen judge (judging-v7, D354), unchanged. Once a day it judges every judged question whose\n   evidence changed, citing the evidence it used. On a question's due day production's closing step records the final answer (D567).\n\n### What the ladder step decides\n\n- **One judged question per thesis**, the part the thesis's outcome names. Every other part is listed as not judged and shown beside\n  the answer (D397). A second side of the same bet is context, not a second question (D436).\n- **An event** has at most three outcomes: it happened, it is ruled out, or still open by the due day. Code writes \"still open\"\n  (D409). Ruling out is stated as who decides and what they decide (D416); a proceeding that formally ends without the conclusion\n  asked for is ruled out (D420).\n- **A figure** has exactly five ranges set against a reference, with the favourable end registered and its reason; code orders and\n  labels the ranges (D398, carrying D186). An amount already reported may be the reference, labelled as that and never as the market's\n  expectation (D411). Where the texts do not settle it, an amount the company owes or guarantees has the lower end favourable (D413).\n- **The owner**: the company, or a theme. A government's permission or ban on a class of product belongs to the theme (D418).\n- **The due day**: for an event, a stated date or the last day of the thesis's period (D403); for a year's figure, code sets it to the\n  fiscal year-end plus 60 days for a company and the calendar year-end plus 90 days for a theme (D414).\n- **A decline** is allowed with its reason: no main part, a rolling twelve months with no publication day (D417), or no theme that\n  covers the matter. An answer refused twice by code writes no question (D405). A thesis without a judged question is kept, and the\n  links to it are counted, not judged.\n\n### Where it stands\n\n- **Built and frozen as it ran in the fourth Stage A** (D426). A second look by the model at its own answer was tested and not adopted\n  (D425).\n- **Gate 1 (Stage A) is not met.** In the fourth run on Alex's 44 theses, lines 2 and 6 were met, line 5 on two of three readings, and\n  lines 1, 3 and 4 missed by 1, 2 and 3 answers of 131. Every later reading on fresh topics also missed it (nine readings in all).\n- **Gate 2 (Stage B) is not met.** Three draws, 236 judgements: unsupported figure answers 3.4%, 3.2% and 10.5% against a limit of\n  12.7–14.4%, and yes/no answers 0.9%, 0.8% and 4.9% against 5% (both met); the control line could not be exercised (4 comparable\n  controls of 8 needed), and the strictest reading of the last-answer line missed (3 of 24)\n  ([result](step4-2026-11-10/stage_b/STAGE_B_RESULT.md)). Re-sending the judge cannot close the control shortfall (D576, D592).\n- **On the product's page** the judged question, its answer choices and the parts not judged are shown without any invented direction\n  (D570–D573).\n\n## 8. How the system is measured, and where it stands\n\n### The measuring stick\n\n- **One frozen ruler** for historical comparison (AGENTS §7.1; D261, D262): the signed extraction standard with its gold\n  (golden-v5), the 22 seed questions, and reader B's judgement gold at the window end 2026-07-14. None of its files changes. Since\n  D372 future evidence scoring counts the gold's core and supporting entries as helpful and its not-kept entries as unhelpful.\n- **Each step's own acceptance protocol.** Steps 1 and 2 were accepted on 50 fresh articles no run had used, read against the source\n  by two readers and a third on disputes (D386). The ladder step is read on stored theses against Alex's six lines by two model\n  families (D400). The judge's answers are each read alone by both families against a baseline, with planted wrong answers (D428).\n- **The traces themselves.** A score is a lead. Every measurement is followed by reading the raw answers beside their sources.\n- **Use on unseen news** (gate 4, D484): readers first list the big matters in a week of news, then check what each system covered,\n  whether its last answers are supported, and which key facts never reached an answer.\n\n### Where each step stands\n\n| Step | Its lines | Result | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Step 1 (c8) | Helpful kept; wrongly opened theses \"50 篇里最多 2 个\" (D386). | 50 fresh articles, one draw: helpful 132 of 139 (core 83 of 83); wrongly opened 1 of 10. Dev, three draws on golden-v5: helpful 214, 211, 212 of 234. Weak point: link clutter (19 of 34 disputed links weak, 2 wrong). | Section 12; [round log](step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md) §§40–44 |\n| Step 2 | The three lines of section 5. | Search 141 of 143; clear wrong merges 1 of 57; details kept 22 = 22 (one draw). | Section 5; D386 |\n| Step 4a (gate 1) | Alex's six lines (D400). | Not met in nine readings. | Section 7 |\n| Step 4b (gate 2) | D428. | Not met: controls unexercised; strictest last-answer reading missed. | Section 7 |\n| End to end (gate 3) | D427, D431. | Owed. Diagnostics so far: seeded 18, 18, 17 against 17, 15, 16 of 23, inconclusive (D392–D394); same-day July census 2, 1, 0 against 1, 1, 1 settled agreements, every loss at opening (D451). | [Comparison](e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/) |\n| Use on unseen news (gate 4) | Proposed, not Alex's. | One fresh week, one draw: big matters covered 2 of 5 against 4 of 5; last answers supported 6 of 7 against 10 of 15; \"not clear\" (D489). | [Week result](../docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/week/WEEK-RESULT.md) |\n\n### What the measurements say\n\n- **In the end-to-end diagnostics, answers were lost before judging.** On the seven graded ruler questions no eligible judgement\n  disagreed with reader B in either system; every loss came at opening: the question was not opened, or not in a shape the ruler\n  grades (D395, D451). Where it was given the facts, the judge reasoned as the expert readers did (section 1.2). The judge's own test\n  on the new system's questions has still not passed (gate 2).\n- **The core problem is forming answerable questions** (D559): a worthwhile matter is left without an answerable question, or a\n  delivered question asks a materially different or unresolvable thing. No single cause is proven.\n- **Model readers disagree.** Since 2026-11-10 every quality judgement has come from model readers, and their two families disagree.\n  Alex's own marks on a pilot deck come first ([decision page](../docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/DECISION-PAGE-2026-11-16.md),\n  choice 1).\n\nThe records of each round are in the step logs linked above and in\n[`docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/LEAN-LOG.md`](../docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/LEAN-LOG.md). The first Step 1\ninvestigations of 2026-11-06 and 11-07 are kept word for word in [PRD-HISTORY.md](PRD-HISTORY.md).\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/carried-library/RESULT.md docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/carried-run/RESULT.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/carried-library/RESULT.md docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/carried-run/RESULT.md'","aggregated_output":"# The small comparison of D559: the same 11 articles read from a kept library and from empty (D561), 2026-11-16\n\n**Answer: on these 11 selected articles, the read with last week's library re-opened none of the four questions it already\nheld and filed the identified facts under them; it opened 4 questions where the read from empty opened 16, none of them on a\nmatter the library held (both reader families). It opened three of the four missing matters the empty read opened (not the\nequity-investment commitments), so by the rule written before the run the result sits between \"supports going on\" and\n\"fails\", and is reported as it is. One draw; nothing about final answers.**\n\nRun: `poc/data/clean-carried-library-2026-11-16/` (22 of 22 reads complete, one call each, no resend; ledger $0.427 of $1.00).\nDesign and proofs: `DESIGN.md`, `proofs/`. My own read stored before the readers': `OWN-READ.md`. Readers (two families,\nblind to which read had the library in so far as the packet allows: the library shown is part of a read): `brief-readers.md`,\n`codex-readers.jsonl`, `claude-readers.jsonl`; tally `tally_readers.py` → `tally-readers.txt`.\n\n## The rule written before the run\n\n| Line | Result |\n|---|---|\n| Re-opens a held question in two or more of the four same-question cases | **0 of 4** (supply commitments, Hugging Face, Rubin's ramp, 2026 spending) |\n| Drops the matter's statements where the empty read kept them, in two or more | **0 of 4** by my own reading before the readers (`OWN-READ.md`): 1, 1, 2, 1 statements filed under the held question against 1, 1, 2, 1 kept by the empty read; the readers judged the homes, not a fact-by-fact comparison. Article 15's sentence holds both $800bn for 2026 and $1.3tn for 2027; it went under the 2026 question, and 2027 is tracked by neither read |\n| Opens two or fewer of the absent cases the empty read opens | opens **3 of the empty read's 4** (Groq's rack, the B200 price, Reflection AI; not the equity commitments), plus memory prices, which the empty read did not open |\n| Supports going on only if, besides the two lines above, the absent cases open as in the empty read | **not met** (3 of 4); not failed either: between the two, reported as it is |\n\n## What the readers say (both families unless split)\n\n| | Empty | Carried |\n|---|---|---|\n| Questions opened | 16 | 4 |\n| Of them, on a matter the library shown already held | 0 (no library) | **0** |\n| Worth holding | 12 yes; 3 yes/unsure; 1 yes/no (global AI spending as a share of U.S. GDP) | 1 yes; 3 yes/unsure (memory prices, Groq's rack, the B200 price) |\n| Statements filed under a held question | n/a | 27: right 17, wrong 2, unsure 2, split 6 |\n| Reader-listed entries of a matter deserving a question that the read neither opened nor held (both families' lists added per article and read; names not merged, so not distinct matters) | 25 | 18 |\n\n**The two homes both families call wrong:** customers' share of NVIDIA's receivables filed under the credit-support total\n(article 173); the CFO's 70% growth outlook filed under supply commitments (article 206; D508's readers had counted the same\nkind of statement as bearing on that question, so the line is disputed across readings).\n\n**What the carried read lost:** a question on a DOJ or FTC action against the Groq deal (opened from empty; the stored October\nrun did not open it either; one family names it missing for the carried read, the other did not mark its worth above\n\"unsure\"). The equity-investment commitments were not opened; their facts went under the held credit-support question, whose\nown outcome reads \"versus the equity investments it has made\", and both families call those four homes right: the held\nquestion's scope carries into the filing, and the separate equity-commitments question the empty read opened was not opened.\n\n**A gap the readers name in both conditions:** questions on the company's own numbers. The Claude-family reader names the\n$108bn quarterly outlook, the 70% growth outlook for fiscal 2028 and the gross-margin outlook cut to 72-73% on several\narticles; the Codex-family reader names gross margins and memory costs, 2028 growth, customer concentration and the funding\nof investments and payouts. These are missing questions, not missing facts: the figures were mostly kept, and in the carried\nread the $108bn outlook was filed under the China question. It is the standing product rule (D382, D384) and the item Alex\nruled on (D505: such figures should reach the judgements of related questions), not something this test changes.\n\n## Limits\n\nEverything above is one read of these articles from a fixed library, not behaviour over a changing library. One draw; 11 articles chosen because they opened topics (not a sample of the week); a seven-topic library one week old,\nitself opened from empty; the step's read of each article from a fixed library (no carry-over between articles); no\nmatching, answer choices or judging run. The empty reads differ from the stored October run of the same articles (12 and 35\nopened nothing; 15 opened a 2026-only question): run-to-run variation, one draw each.\n# The product condition, end to end over two weeks with the library kept: the reading (D563), 2026-11-16\n\n**Answer: in this one draw, with the library of week 1 held, the six questions week 2 opened duplicated none it held (both\nfamilies), and the step that writes answer choices wrote them for 12 of 13 topics in all three attempts, but the choices do not meet Alex's six lines (gate 1: 7 or 5 of 13\ntopics right against a scaled 11). Of the 12 last answers, none is unsupported by both families and 6 are contested\nby at least one of three readers, for several different reasons. The company's own numbers have no home by product rule, and\nneither reader family predicted that any not delivered would change one of the 12 answers; two statements over-read their\npassages; the product's page could not show the new system's library (fixed at the writer, D564). One draw, one company,\none fortnight; week 2's later steps were regenerated with week 1's, not a live two-week history.**\n\nRun: `poc/data/clean-carried-run-2026-11-16/` (week 1 the stored run of 2026-09-22..28 from empty; week 2 2026-09-29..10-05, 241\nnever-sent articles, read from week 1's library; the later steps regenerated over the fortnight; ledger $3.476 of $6.00, no\nrefusal). Plan frozen before any answer was read (`PLAN.md`, sha256 in `PLAN.sha256`); my own read stored first (`OWN-READ.md`).\n\n## What the run did\n\n| | Week 1 (from empty) | Week 2 (library kept) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Articles | 235 | 241 |\n| Topics opened | 7 | 6 |\n| Kept statements with no link | 389 of 569 (68%) | 339 of 626 (54%) |\n\nAnswer choices over the fortnight: **12 of 13 topics written in all three attempts**; the one declined in all three is\n\"venture funding for physical AI companies\" (no listed theme covers it; the two opening readers split on its worth, my own read says not worth holding). Written is not right: read against\nAlex's six lines (reading A below), they do not meet them. For comparison, from an empty start: the week of 10-06, 4 of 13\ntopics declined at least once, 2 every time, and agreed faults beyond refusals; July, 21 of 104 declined in two or three attempts.\nDifferent weeks and articles: an observation, not a measured difference.\n\n## Reading A: Alex's six lines on the fortnight's answer choices (`poc/data/clean-carried-run-2026-11-16/stage-a/report.txt`)\n\nThe October tooling unchanged: two families apart (39 valid rows each), 24 sends disputed, a third reader of each family on 34\npoints; every unresolved point counted both ways. **Gate 1 is not met:** line 1 missed (3 or 5 sends), line 2 missed (7), line 3\nmissed (4 or 7), line 4 met (the only refusals, robotics funding ×3, justified), line 5 missed (right 7 or 5 of 13 against a scaled\n11; by written choices 6 or 4 against 9), line 6 not measured. Fault kinds: a stated plan left out of an event's reference (not\nshown to the judge; its effect untraced); a due day before the report that settles the event (the Rubin revenue question, upheld\nby both third readers; the CPO case split); \"and\" in the question against \"or\" in the Yes rung (MIIT, disputed among third\nreaders); parts left unlisted; a question leaning on an unnamed lab. The October and July readings used other articles and draws:\nthis does not isolate the effect of the library.\n\n**The due-date class, sized** (`due_day_census.py` → `due-day-census.txt`; a pattern screen, flagged cases read by hand): across\nevery stored written event answer, the only answers naming a periodic report as the sole settling material with the due day at\nthe period's end are six attempts of one matter (Rubin's FY2027 revenue, the same week-1 topic in two readings). Not a reason to\nopen a new version of the step.\n\n## Reading 1: week 2's openings (two families; `tally-openings.txt`)\n\n- **Re-opened a held question: 0 of 6** by both families. Same matter, another shape: capital spending for 2027 beside 2026 (both;\n  a year of its own, D38), the residual-value guarantee beside the credit-support total (one family; a signing question, D388).\n- Worth holding: 2 yes by both (capital spending 2027; the residual-value guarantee); 4 split yes/no (robotics funding, U.S.\n  gigawatts added in 2026, DRAM contract prices 2026, co-packaged-optics switch volumes).\n- Controls on 26 articles (the 6 openers and 20 drawn by seed): lost facts named by both families 0; wrong homes named by both 2\n  (an investment return under credit support; Micron's customer commitments under NVIDIA's supply commitments).\n- Lost questions both families name again and again: **the company's own numbers** (the quarter's revenue against the $108bn\n  guide, fiscal 2028 growth against 70%, margins, earnings) and **the $150bn buyback**. The first is the product rule (D382,\n  D384; D505: such figures should reach the judgements of related questions, not be questions of their own; not built).\n\n## Reading 2: every last answer (two families, planted and confirmed controls; `tally-answers.txt`)\n\n- **12 questions: supported by both 9; unsupported by both 0; split 3** (memory prices 2026: insufficient/supported; the credit\n  support total: the $108.5bn cap read as the amount, unsupported/supported; 2026 capital spending: $820bn by adding Amazon's\n  raise to the $800bn reference, unsupported/supported).\n- Controls: 4 planted wrong answers caught by both families (4 of 4; the plants are conspicuous contradictions between a choice\n  and its own reasons); 4 confirmed supported controls accepted by both (4 of 4; these are 4 of the 12 real answers, not\n  additional ones).\n- Statement fidelity, verified against the stored passages: `e590` says the Hugging Face CEO \"said he had just sold his company\"\n  and that the acquisition \"had been completed\"; the passage is TechCrunch's parenthetical, and completion is an inference (both\n  families flag it). `e279` labels Micron's November quarter \"fiscal Q1 2026\", copied from the article's title, not its passage\n  (one family). The first step's summary added what the passage does not hold.\n- The separate control reader (8 candidates) marked 3 not supported (U.S. gigawatts, memory prices, CPO volumes) and 1 unsure (the\n  residual-value guarantee). Across all three readers, **6 of 12 answers are uncontested** (supply commitments, China's Blackwell\n  approval, Rubin's revenue, the Hugging Face closing, MIIT's approval, 2027 capital spending) and 6 contested, for several\n  reasons: a cap read as the amount, a possible double count, qualitative evidence used to choose a numeric band, an event\n  forecast, and an answer-choice interpretation. Cases to trace one by one; not one proven mechanism.\n\n## Reading 3: did the answers get the facts that could change them? (a reader lead, not a test; `claude-sources.jsonl`, `codex-sources.jsonl`)\n\nFive questions drawn by seed (supply commitments, China's Blackwell approval, 2026 and 2027 capital spending, the residual-value\nguarantee), each read with its last request and up to six of the fortnight's articles on it. No family named an undelivered fact\nthat would move an answer; a few that would change its confidence. **The reading fell short of its frozen plan:** the packet\nshowed the request but not the last answer itself; it had no separate negative control; the readers listed absent facts\nthemselves instead of a code classification of every listed fact. The positive control (PRD 10's custom-chip group) was caught by\nthe Claude-family reader and missed by the Codex-family reader. A lead from one family on five questions, not a result.\n\n## Reading 4: did the company's own figures reach the questions they bear on? (D564; `figures_census.py`, `tally-figures.txt`)\n\nBy the statements' own fields (party NVIDIA, a typed financial figure): 270 kept statements in 61 groups of repeats. Against each of\nthe 12 judged questions' last request, the groups not shown and dated by its day: 46 to 61 a question. Two families apart read\nevery question with its whole last request and answer, the custom-chip question of July with its five facts mixed in as a positive\ncontrol and one statement already shown to the Rubin question as a negative.\n\n- **No pair that would move an answer, by either family.** Pairs named as changing only the confidence: NVIDIA's sales to the\n  largest cloud companies for the two capital-spending questions (both families on the 2027 one); revenue per gigawatt and\n  data-center revenue as a cross-check of U.S. gigawatts (Claude family only); cash-flow figures for the Hugging Face and\n  residual-value questions (Codex family only, the Claude family \"neither\").\n- **Controls.** Negative: neither family named the statement already shown. Positive: the Claude family named the two Broadcom\n  revenue figures as moving the answer (as they did, 9 of 9 sends, in PRD 10's test). The Codex family named nothing in the main\n  reading because the packet dated the control facts \"2026-07\" and left the negative's day empty: **my packet's fault**; on a\n  control packet with the real days it named four as confidence and the fifth \"cannot tell\", none as moving the answer. So a Codex-family \"no effect\" is weak evidence, as\n  in reading 3; the Claude family caught the control both times.\n- **What it shows:** among the selected figures and questions, neither family predicted an answer-choice change; the control and\n  selection limits leave a delivery gap unresolved. Limits (council round 12): the selector takes only statements whose party is\n  NVIDIA and whose typed measure matches a fixed list, not every financial fact; it drops a repeat group when any member was\n  shown, so a repeat's own details in an unshown member are not read (partly shown groups exist); readers saw summaries and\n  figures, not the source passages; a reader's \"would move\" is a prediction until a send tests it. Reading 1's largest missing\n  matter (the company's own figures) is first a matter of which questions exist (D382, D384: figures are evidence, not questions).\n- **Gate 1, per topic** (`faults_by_topic.py`, `faults-by-topic.txt`): the most frequent failing mark is a stated plan left out of an\n  event's reference (7 attempts, 3 topics), which is not shown to the judge; fixing it alone would make 2 more topics right\n  (9 or 7 of 13 against 11, a paper upper bound if that mark alone changed). No one kind of fault holds the gap.\n\n## Found on the way\n\n**Integration** (`INTEGRATION-CHECK.md`): the product's page server shows the new system's stored library as empty: the read side\nplaces questions in time by completed days, which the new system's writer never records. Gate 5's first concrete defect.\n\n## Limits\n\nOne draw; one company; one fortnight; week 2's later steps were regenerated with week 1's (not a live system's history); the\nreaders are two model families and one separate model reader; reading 3 is five questions. Nothing here compares the two systems\nor passes a gate.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
