{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_29","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"sqlite3 'file:poc/data/clean-lean-week-2026-11-14/week-r2/c1/step1/trace.sqlite?mode=ro' '.schema'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_29","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"sqlite3 'file:poc/data/clean-lean-week-2026-11-14/week-r2/c1/step1/trace.sqlite?mode=ro' '.schema'\"","aggregated_output":"CREATE TABLE run (manifest text not null, settings text not null, source_items text not null, initial_theses text not null);\nCREATE TABLE trace (sequence integer primary key, input_id text not null, before_state text not null,\n            request text not null, raw_response text, result text, after_state text, status text not null, error text, reserved_usd real,\n            retry_reason text);\nCREATE TABLE model_calls (\n  call_id integer primary key autoincrement, purpose text, config_version text, config_checksum text, model text,\n  request_sha256 text unique, request_json text, response_json text, answer_json text, prompt_chars integer,\n  input_tokens integer, output_tokens integer, cost_usd real, seconds real, created_at text\n);\nCREATE TABLE sqlite_sequence(name,seq);\nCREATE INDEX model_calls_sha on model_calls(request_sha256);\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_30","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import sqlite3; p=\\\"file:poc/data/clean-lean-week-2026-11-14/week-r2/c1/step1/trace.sqlite?mode=ro\\\"; c=sqlite3.connect(p,uri=True); rows=c.execute(\\\"select sequence,input_id,request,raw_response,result,after_state from trace where request like ? and (request like ? or request like ?) limit 2\\\",(\\\"%45%\\\",\\\"%70%\\\",\\\"%2028%\\\"));\nfor row in rows:\n print(\\\"SEQUENCE\\\",row[0],\\\"ID\\\",row[1]);\n for label,val in zip((\\\"REQUEST\\\",\\\"RAW\\\",\\\"RESULT\\\",\\\"STATE\\\"),row[2:]):\n  print(label, str(val)[:16000])'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_30","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import sqlite3; p=\\\"file:poc/data/clean-lean-week-2026-11-14/week-r2/c1/step1/trace.sqlite?mode=ro\\\"; c=sqlite3.connect(p,uri=True); rows=c.execute(\\\"select sequence,input_id,request,raw_response,result,after_state from trace where request like ? and (request like ? or request like ?) limit 2\\\",(\\\"%45%\\\",\\\"%70%\\\",\\\"%2028%\\\"));\nfor row in rows:\n print(\\\"SEQUENCE\\\",row[0],\\\"ID\\\",row[1]);\n for label,val in zip((\\\"REQUEST\\\",\\\"RAW\\\",\\\"RESULT\\\",\\\"STATE\\\"),row[2:]):\n  print(label, str(val)[:16000])'\"","aggregated_output":"SEQUENCE 3 ID gnews:a31be66f8ce2e98461d3206c\nREQUEST {\"model\": \"deepseek-flash\", \"messages\": [{\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"ROLE\\n\\nYou are a senior hedge-fund analyst reading one source item for the company in scope (current_entity); \\\"the company\\\" below is that\\ncompany. The research already tracked for it is listed in open_theses. Your first job is evidence: decide by the rules below which\\nstatements in the item are material, then link each one to every thesis it informs: an open thesis that covers it, a new thesis you\\npropose, or both. Your second job is new theses: the important research questions this item raises that no open thesis\\ncovers. Every new thesis comes with evidence from this item. A material statement that no open thesis covers and that is not\\nitself a big matter (section 3) is returned with empty links: code keeps it in the company's record without links and links it\\nwhen a thesis that covers it opens. Never open a thesis only to give a statement a home.\\n\\nINPUT\\n\\n- current_entity and company_tags: the company in scope and the companies the item is tagged with. Code assigns the entity; do not\\n  invent one. Evidence can describe another party when it informs research on the company.\\n- open_theses: the identity, question, tracked outcome and period of each thesis already tracked. They tell you where evidence belongs\\n  and whether an idea is already covered; they are not evidence. You do not see the evidence already stored; a later step compares\\n  new evidence with history.\\n- source_item: the complete text and its source metadata.\\n\\n1. EVIDENCE: WHAT TO KEEP\\n\\n- A material statement is one of four kinds. (1) The company's own figure, guidance or decision: \\\"same-store sales rose 4.2% in the\\n  quarter\\\"; \\\"management guided full-year revenue to $12\\u201313 billion\\\"; \\\"the board approved a $10 billion buyback\\\". (2) A customer's,\\n  supplier's, competitor's or partner's action or reported figure with a size, date or name that bears on the company's demand,\\n  supply, input costs, competition, prices or products: \\\"the largest customer said it will cut orders by a fifth next year\\\"; \\\"a bank\\n  chose a rival's system\\\"; \\\"a software firm is integrating the company's model into its government systems\\\"; \\\"the main supplier said\\n  its capacity will double by 2027\\\"; \\\"a rival cut list prices 15% in Europe\\\"; \\\"the largest customer reported its order backlog doubled\\n  to $40 billion\\\". An action is a step the item reports as news: taken, decided or planned (an order, a contract, a selection, an\\n  integration under way, an expansion, a cut, a price change, a deployment plan). A party that already uses, hosts, runs or has built\\n  on the company's products, and the benefits it describes from that use (faster, cheaper, more accurate), describe an arrangement in\\n  place, which is background; its own revenue, capacity, orders or contracted amounts are figures under (2) (\\\"a hospital chain uses\\n  the company's scheduling software and reports shorter waits\\\"). (3) A named party's new claim or estimate about the company or its\\n  industry, with or without a figure, whether an analyst, a customer's or rival's executive or an official makes it: \\\"the CFO said\\n  plants built a decade ago still run at full capacity\\\"; \\\"an analyst raised next year's EPS estimate to $4.10\\\"; \\\"an analyst said the\\n  bank's loan losses next quarter will come in well above consensus\\\"; \\\"a short seller accused the company of overstating receivables\\n  by $2 billion\\\". (4) A policy or regulatory decision or\\n  step, including one still in force that the item reports: \\\"the regulator approved the drug for a second indication\\\"; \\\"the\\n  government required licences for exports of the product\\\"; \\\"the regulator still bars sales of the drug in the country\\\".\\n- Go through the item from its first paragraph to its last, including paragraphs about matters other than the headline. The four\\n  kinds describe what material statements usually look like; they do not decide what is kept. Take a statement when an analyst\\n  following the company would write it in this week's notes because it makes some question's answer more or less likely, and no row\\n  of the closing check at the end of this request rejects it; a row's \\\"it does not cover\\\" clause sends the statement back to that test\\n  and keeps it only where the row says so. Most of an item is background, but a page with eight statements that pass yields eight\\n  evidence items, not a selection.\\n\\n2. LINK EACH STATEMENT TO ITS THESES\\n\\n- EXISTING: if an open thesis covers the statement, link it to that thesis's exact supplied ID, including contrary facts. Do not\\n  return or rename the thesis's description.\\n- NEW: if the statement raises a research question that merits separate tracking and no open or newly proposed thesis covers it,\\n  propose a thesis (section 3) and link the statement to it.\\n- NO THESIS YET: if no open or newly proposed thesis covers a statement you judge material, and the matter is not big enough by\\n  its own scale to merit a thesis (section 3), return it with empty links; it is kept without links.\\n- CONDITION: a statement of the company's own financial figures (a reported result or balance, its guidance, or a named party's\\n  or consensus estimate) lists its useful figures in figures. Its results, guidance and estimates, margins included, inform an open thesis about its revenue or earnings\\n  path when they bear on that path; a different period is not on its own a reason to leave them unlinked. When no open or new\\n  thesis covers it, its links stay empty. A figure is useful when it can change what an analyst\\n  expects of the company's revenue, profitability, funding or credit risk, or competitive position: a retailer's quarterly\\n  same-store sales, an airline's guidance for next year's unit costs and analysts' consensus earnings for a bank are; a cash-rich\\n  company's cash balance or cash flow usually changes none of these when the item ties it to nothing, while a heavily indebted\\n  firm's cash burn changes its credit risk. The share price, a target price and other companies' figures are never conditions.\\n- Link one statement to every thesis it informs, and to none that its own relevance says it does not inform; use the narrowest\\n  relevant thesis only when choosing between theses that cover the same thing.\\n- Match by the underlying question or outcome, subject and scope, and time when relevant, not by word overlap. A different wording,\\n  source, forecast or direction alone does not make a new thesis. Related questions can be separate when their outcomes matter\\n  independently.\\n- Do not exclude evidence solely because it concerns a related period, population or product variant. Assess whether it can inform\\n  the open question, preserving the difference in the record. Do not present a forecast for a later year as a forecast for the\\n  thesis's year. If another open thesis covers the evidence more directly, consider that thesis as well; do not assume different\\n  periods are automatically the same thesis or automatically unrelated.\\n\\nExamples of linking evidence to research:\\n\\n- Supply chain: an open thesis asks whether a manufacturer's production is constrained. A named supplier announces a capacity\\n  expansion for a component the manufacturer uses. Link the announcement as a potential supply signal; it does not establish\\n  allocations or deliveries.\\n- Geopolitics: an open thesis asks whether an exporter can sell in a specified market. The relevant government proposes a licensing\\n  restriction on that product. Link the proposal as evidence about market access; a proposal is not an enacted restriction.\\n- Macro: an open thesis asks whether a company's borrowing costs will rise. The item reports that its floating-rate debt is tied to a\\n  benchmark that has risen. Link the benchmark movement and the stated exposure; do not calculate the expense change unless the\\n  source supports it.\\n- Competition: an open thesis asks whether a company can retain customers in a product market. A named competitor cuts the price of a\\n  competing product. Link the price cut as competitive evidence; do not assert lost customers or share without support.\\n\\n3. WHEN TO PROPOSE A NEW THESIS\\n\\nA thesis is a question a senior analyst would hold a card for: its answer could change a position because it could change the\\ncompany's revenue, profit, cash or credit risk, or competitive position in a way you can name; public material will settle it on a\\ndate; and no open thesis already asks it. A thesis asks one quantity for one period, or one event. It opens for one of four reasons, a closed set; a matter that fits none of them is\\nevidence, kept and linked to the theses it informs or left unlinked, never a thesis:\\n- product_node: when the generation of the main product that carries the company's value reaches the step that brings revenue,\\n  usually first shipments; announcements and samples are its evidence, and only milestones an analyst bets on separately (a drug's\\n  trial result and its approval) are separate theses (\\\"will the carmaker's new platform start deliveries in 2026?\\\";\\n  \\\"will the drugmaker's next-generation inhaler, the product line that carries its value, reach pharmacies by mid-2027?\\\").\\n- binding_obligation: an obligation the company takes on in its own name, large enough to change its revenue, profit, cash or credit\\n  risk: guaranteeing, backing or giving credit support for another party's debt, lease, revenue or capacity, lending to it,\\n  committing to buy capacity or services, or a sized stake or acquisition not yet completed. One already undertaken is tracked by its\\n  amount at the next annual report; one not yet signed by whether it is signed by a check date within 18 months of the item (\\\"how much will the\\n  utility have guaranteed for its project companies' loans at its next annual report?\\\"; \\\"will the retailer sign a guarantee for its\\n  franchisees' leases, large enough to change its credit risk, by the check date?\\\"). Its own borrowing or leases and a completed\\n  or unsized investment are not obligations.\\n- shared_quantity: a quantity or price outside any one company that several companies depend on, for this or next calendar year:\\n  several companies' combined output or spending, a contract price, an interest rate (\\\"the largest container lines' combined capacity\\n  added in 2026\\\"; \\\"next year's average contract price of a raw material\\\"). Another population or wording of the same aggregate is\\n  evidence for it.\\n- policy_stage: the step a policy or regulatory process reaches, naming who decides (\\\"will the regulator approve the drug for a second\\n  indication?\\\"; \\\"will the government require licences for the product's exports by a date?\\\").\\n\\n- Open only matters an analyst would bet on separately; small matters open no thesis and their statements are kept as evidence. A\\n  customer's deployment plan is evidence for the company's demand and product theses, unless it changes the company's own cash or\\n  credit risk, which is judged as an obligation.\\n- Give the date the item supports. When it gives none, use the date the reason fixes and say how it was set: an obligation already\\n  undertaken, its next annual report; an unsigned obligation, a check date within 18 months of the item; a pending regulatory\\n  decision, the current fiscal year-end. A shared quantity takes its release date and a product event the date its outcome can be\\n  checked, only when the item supports one. A question no date can settle is rewritten or not opened.\\n- Do not open a thesis about: an executive appointment or organisational change; the company's own borrowing or leases as an\\n  obligation; a buyback authorisation or dividend declaration; a financial measure's level, growth, margin or guidance for any period,\\n  on its own; an undated moat or strategy; a feature, content or software update, price point, regional service or variant of a\\n  product already on sale; another estimate, source or wording of what an open thesis tracks; an annual quantity after next year (an\\n  obligation excepted); shareholder votes, bylaws or routine litigation procedure. Their statements are evidence for the theses they\\n  inform, or left unlinked.\\n- Before proposing a new thesis, name its reason (opens_as), the public material that will settle it (settled_by), and the closest\\n  open thesis with why an analyst would bet on the two separately because their answers can move apart; with no open thesis, say so.\\n  If the two cannot move apart, link the evidence to every open thesis it informs instead.\\n\\nCounterexamples:\\n\\n- \\\"The company's outlook is interesting\\\" or \\\"the industry may change\\\" is too vague: it names no outcome later evidence can inform.\\n- If an open thesis tracks sales growth, a revised sales forecast is evidence for it; do not open a second thesis to repeat it.\\n- A customer expanding capacity informs demand; it is not a thesis of its own, and not a claim that the supplier has received orders.\\n- \\\"Is the stock attractively valued?\\\" is an investment conclusion, not a research question about the business.\\n\\n4. HOW TO WRITE EVIDENCE\\n\\n- One evidence item records one statement, and that statement is what you judge: a figure, date or comparison in the neighbouring\\n  words does not make another claim in the same passage new or material. A statement told in several sentences of the item, with its\\n  terms, figures and conditions, is one item.\\n- Write a standalone summary: who did or said what, how much and for which period, naming the measure even when a neighbouring\\n  sentence gives it. Preserve conditions, uncertainty and attribution. Return every passage the summary rests on, each copied\\n  verbatim and contiguously from the item, without translation; do not join distant passages with an ellipsis.\\n- Name the actor in the summary and identify the original party whose action, figure or claim it is. Give the reporting speaker when\\n  available. The item's publisher and publication date come from its source metadata.\\n- Use only this item's information. Distinguish forecasts, allegations and unconfirmed reports from confirmed outcomes. Report plans\\n  as plans; do not infer committed funding, orders, deliveries or revenue.\\n- For each item, return its evidence tier, the original statement date and the period the statement describes. These are separate\\n  pieces of information.\\n\\nEVIDENCE TIER\\n\\nClassify the statement by what is confirmed, not how important it is:\\n\\n- rumour: the item attributes the claim to unnamed sources or calls it unconfirmed.\\n- fact: an official action, published actual figure or completed transaction, including an announcement or completed transaction\\n  reported by an article.\\n- party_statement: a company's guidance, management statement or official's remarks. A forecast is a statement, not a completed\\n  outcome.\\n- third_party_view: an analyst's, journalist's or expert's opinion or estimate.\\n\\nApply the first matching category in that order. An unnamed-source or unconfirmed claim remains a rumour even if it describes an\\naction or number. If none matches and the claim is unattributed, use rumour. The tier is not an importance score.\\n\\nEVIDENCE TIME\\n\\n- statement_date: the original statement's full day in YYYY-MM-DD when known; otherwise null. Do not use the article date as the\\n  original date unless the statement was made in the originating party's own release, filing, call or analysis. Do not assign that\\n  date to an older statement quoted there.\\n- statement_date_text: the original date or partial date as stated, if present; otherwise null. Preserve a month or relative date\\n  without inventing a day. A forecast horizon or an occasion is not a date.\\n- evidence_period: the period the reported fact, plan or forecast covers, as supported by the item; otherwise null. This is not the\\n  statement date.\\n\\nReturn the theses an\nRAW {\"id\": \"f9b709d0-add1-487b-8990-857c9ed3bc81\", \"object\": \"chat.completion\", \"created\": 1794688451, \"model\": \"deepseek-flash\", \"choices\": [{\"index\": 0, \"message\": {\"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": \"{\\\"new_theses\\\": [], \\\"evidence\\\": []}\", \"reasoning_content\": \"Let me read the item carefully.\\n\\nThe item is a CNBC options-trading piece about Nvidia. Key content:\\n\\n- Nvidia shares rallied 24% in 2026, five straight sessions, stock 3.5% below all-time closing high of $235.74 in May.\\n- Catalysts: Jensen Huang expected to attend state dinner for Chinese President Xi Jinping when Xi travels to Washington to meet President Trump this Thursday. AI expected main topic.\\n- September 30: Micron expected to report fiscal Q4 earnings, a barometer for AI memory demand that may have ripple effects on Nvidia.\\n- Nvidia's implied volatility near lowest level of past year.\\n- Trade recommendation: reverse iron condor.\\n- Monday: AMD surged more than 9% to cross $1 trillion market cap, Intel and Arm each jumped double digits on renewed enthusiasm for AI chip demand.\\n\\nNow, what's material under the four kinds?\\n\\n(1) Company's own figure, guidance, decision: Nvidia shares rallied 24% in 2026 — that's a share price move, which is not kept (\\\"a share-price move ... is not kept\\\"). The forward multiple falling — valuation, not kept. Implied volatility near lowest — that's options pricing, market-derived; arguably a market observation not a company figure. Not kept? It's a market statistic, not a company financial figure. Section 2 CONDITION: share price, target price are never conditions. Implied volatility is market data about options, not a statement making a question's answer more or less likely about the business. Probably not kept — it's an options-market observation. Hmm, but \\\"it does not cover the news behind the move\\\" — the news here is the catalysts.\\n\\n(2) Another party's action or reported figure with size/date/name bearing on demand, supply, competition, prices, products: \\n- Jensen Huang expected to attend a state dinner for Xi Jinping in Washington this Thursday, with AI a main topic. Is this a material statement? It's a plan/step reported as news — CEO attending a state dinner, a political/diplomatic event. Hmm, does it bear on Nvidia's demand/competition? Potentially: US-China AI discussions affecting Nvidia's China export access. But is it a \\\"policy or regulatory decision or step\\\"? A state dinner isn't a policy step. Is it \\\"a named party's new claim or estimate about the company\\\"? No. Executive travel plans... The rule \\\"Do not open a thesis about an executive appointment or organisational change\\\" — not exactly. This is arguably a planned step (CEO traveling to Washington to meet with Trump; attending state dinner). Does it pass the test: would an analyst write it in this week's notes because it makes some question's answer more or less likely? It's a plausible signal on US-China AI policy/Nvidia's China access. But it's vague — expected to attend a state dinner; AI a main topic. No decision, no figure. Hmm. It's reported as a news step (planned attendance). I think it's marginal. It could be retained as evidence about US-China AI policy dynamics potentially affecting Nvidia's China market access — but with no open thesis it'd be unlinked. Would an analyst write it? Probably mention as a catalyst, yes, but the \\\"political or strategic stance with no figure and no decision\\\" exclusion — a state dinner attendance is not a policy step. Hmm, it's arguably a scheduled event, not an action bearing on demand/supply/prices. I lean toward not keeping — it's a vague political-calendar item with no figure, no decision, no outcome bearing on Nvidia's business other than speculation. Actually wait — for NVDA, US-China relations and export controls are hugely material. But the item gives no policy step: just that AI will be discussed at a meeting. That's a strategic/political stance with no decision. I'll drop it. Hmm, but \\\"Do not drop a reported step...\\\" — \\\"Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang is expected to attend a state dinner\\\" — that's a reported planned step, but the step is a social/diplomatic attendance. Does it bear on demand? Not directly. I think it's excluded as a political/strategic matter with no figure and no decision.\\n\\n- Micron expected to report fiscal Q4 earnings on September 30, \\\"a barometer for AI memory demand that may have ripple effects on Nvidia.\\\" That's a scheduled earnings report by a supplier (HBM memory supplier to Nvidia). Is it a material statement? It's not Micron's action with size/date bearing on... it's a scheduled upcoming earnings date. It's a calendar fact, a \\\"catalyst\\\". Does it make a question's answer more or less likely? Not by itself — no figure, no result. It's an upcoming event, not a statement of a figure or action. Hmm. The item gives no actual Micron result. So no evidence content. I'd drop: it's a schedule/calendar note, not a figure, claim, action or policy step. It's an announcement of a future earnings date? Micron hasn't announced a figure. I think this is not material evidence. Though \\\"Micron is expected to report fiscal Q4 earnings\\\" — that's just the earnings calendar. Not kept.\\n\\n- AMD surged more than 9% to cross $1 trillion market cap; Intel and Arm jumped double digits Monday on renewed enthusiasm for AI chip demand. This is a competitor's share-price move — \\\"a share-price move ... is not kept\\\". Also \\\"another company's matter\\\". The underlying news is \\\"renewed enthusiasm for AI chip demand\\\" — that's a general claim of demand strength, common knowledge. Not kept.\\n\\n- Options trade details: a trader's position — \\\"an investor's position or trade\\\" not kept.\\n\\n- Implied volatility near lowest of past year: market data derived from options prices. Is it kept? It's about options pricing, not business fundamentals. An analyst following the company probably wouldn't put it in fundamental notes. It's essentially a market observation/valuation-adjacent. I lean to not keep.\\n\\nHmm, so possibly nothing is kept. But let me reconsider whether any statement passes.\\n\\nConsider the exclusions list: \\\"a share-price move, an investor's position or trade, or the market value\\\" — not kept. So Nvidia rallying 24%, AMD 9% to $1T, Intel/Arm double digits — all share-price moves. The \\\"news behind the move\\\" is \\\"renewed enthusiasm for AI chip demand\\\" — a general claim, common knowledge without figure. Not kept.\\n\\n\\\"a target price or valuation of the company is not kept\\\" — forward multiple falling, implied volatility — valuation-ish. Not kept.\\n\\nSo the answer may be empty lists. Is that right? Let me double-check the trajectory: this is an options-trading column with essentially no fundamental news. The two \\\"catalysts\\\" are a state dinner and Micron's earnings date. Both are calendar/event notes with no content.\\n\\nHmm, but consider whether Micron's fiscal Q4 earnings date merits... it doesn't state a figure or a decision. \\\"Micron is expected to report fiscal Q4 earnings\\\" — the only information is timing. An analyst knows the earnings calendar. Not kept.\\n\\nJensen Huang attending a state dinner: is that \\\"background the analyst already knew\\\"? No, it's news (expected). But is it material enough? Let me weigh: The rule for kind (2) includes \\\"a customer's, supplier's, competitor's or partner's action or reported figure with a size, date or name that bears on the company's demand, supply, input costs, competition, prices or products\\\". Huang is the company's own CEO — so kind (1): \\\"the company's own figure, guidance or decision\\\". Is attending a state dinner a decision? It's a plan. Does it bear on the company's business? The item frames AI as a main topic of Xi-Trump discussion. No decision, no figure. The exclusion: \\\"a political or strategic stance with no figure and no decision\\\" — it does not cover a policy or regulatory step. Attending a dinner is not a policy step. So excluded.\\n\\nTherefore: no evidence, no theses. Return empty lists.\\n\\nBut wait — should I consider that \\\"Nvidia shares have rallied 24% in 2026... sitting just 3.5% below its all-time closing high of $235.74\\\" — share price, excluded. \\\"implied volatility near lowest level of past year\\\" — this is an options-market measure; arguably \\\"market value\\\" adjacent, excluded. Even if kept, no thesis would cover it and it's not a big matter. But it's not about the company's fundamentals.\\n\\nHmm, one could argue implied volatility being at a one-year low is a material market observation for positioning... but the rules exclude share-price and market-value items, and options pricing is similar market data. I'll exclude.\\n\\nSo answer: {\\\"new_theses\\\": [], \\\"evidence\\\": []}.\\n\\nHmm, is that too aggressive? Let me reconsider Micron more carefully. \\\"Then September 30, the following week, Micron is expected to report fiscal Q4 earnings, a barometer for AI memory demand that may have ripple effects on Nvidia.\\\" This is a statement about a supplier's upcoming report — it makes no question's answer more or less likely; it's a statement that information will come. It contains an unargued characterization (\\\"a barometer for AI memory demand\\\"). Excluded.\\n\\nI'm comfortable with empty output.\\n\\nActually, let me reconsider whether the state dinner / Xi-Trump meeting with AI as a main topic is a material statement. For NVDA, US-China AI policy is enormous. But the item states nothing that happened — only expectations. \\\"AI is expected to be a main topic of discussion when Xi meets with Trump.\\\" That's an expectation about an agenda — a political matter with no figure and no decision. Excluded per the \\\"political or strategic stance\\\" row.\\n\\nFinal answer: empty lists.\"}, \"logprobs\": null, \"finish_reason\": \"stop\"}], \"usage\": {\"prompt_tokens\": 7947, \"completion_tokens\": 2119, \"total_tokens\": 10066, \"prompt_tokens_details\": {\"cached_tokens\": 7808}, \"completion_tokens_details\": {\"reasoning_tokens\": 2107}, \"prompt_cache_hit_tokens\": 7808, \"prompt_cache_miss_tokens\": 139}, \"system_fingerprint\": \"bc19ba97cbe016479903222bb1578db6\", \"sent_request\": {\"model\": \"deepseek-flash\", \"messages\": [{\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"ROLE\\n\\nYou are a senior hedge-fund analyst reading one source item for the company in scope (current_entity); \\\"the company\\\" below is that\\ncompany. The research already tracked for it is listed in open_theses. Your first job is evidence: decide by the rules below which\\nstatements in the item are material, then link each one to every thesis it informs: an open thesis that covers it, a new thesis you\\npropose, or both. Your second job is new theses: the important research questions this item raises that no open thesis\\ncovers. Every new thesis comes with evidence from this item. A material statement that no open thesis covers and that is not\\nitself a big matter (section 3) is returned with empty links: code keeps it in the company's record without links and links it\\nwhen a thesis that covers it opens. Never open a thesis only to give a statement a home.\\n\\nINPUT\\n\\n- current_entity and company_tags: the company in scope and the companies the item is tagged with. Code assigns the entity; do not\\n  invent one. Evidence can describe another party when it informs research on the company.\\n- open_theses: the identity, question, tracked outcome and period of each thesis already tracked. They tell you where evidence belongs\\n  and whether an idea is already covered; they are not evidence. You do not see the evidence already stored; a later step compares\\n  new evidence with history.\\n- source_item: the complete text and its source metadata.\\n\\n1. EVIDENCE: WHAT TO KEEP\\n\\n- A material statement is one of four kinds. (1) The company's own figure, guidance or decision: \\\"same-store sales rose 4.2% in the\\n  quarter\\\"; \\\"management guided full-year revenue to $12–13 billion\\\"; \\\"the board approved a $10 billion buyback\\\". (2) A customer's,\\n  supplier's, competitor's or partner's action or reported figure with a size, date or name that bears on the company's demand,\\n  supply, input costs, competition, prices or products: \\\"the largest customer said it will cut orders by a fifth next year\\\"; \\\"a bank\\n  chose a rival's system\\\"; \\\"a software firm is integrating the company's model into its government systems\\\"; \\\"the main supplier said\\n  its capacity will double by 2027\\\"; \\\"a rival cut list prices 15% in Europe\\\"; \\\"the largest customer reported its order backlog doubled\\n  to $40 billion\\\". An action is a step the item reports as news: taken, decided or planned (an order, a contract, a selection, an\\n  integration under way, an expansion, a cut, a price change, a deployment plan). A party that already uses, hosts, runs or has built\\n  on the company's products, and the benefits it describes from that use (faster, cheaper, more accurate), describe an arrangement in\\n  place, which is background; its own revenue, capacity, orders or contracted amounts are figures under (2) (\\\"a hospital chain uses\\n  the company's scheduling software and reports shorter waits\\\"). (3) A named party's new claim or estimate about the company or its\\n  industry, with or without a figure, whether an analyst, a customer's or rival's executive or an official makes it: \\\"the CFO said\\n  plants built a decade ago still run at full capacity\\\"; \\\"an analyst raised next year's EPS estimate to $4.10\\\"; \\\"an analyst said the\\n  bank's loan losses next quarter will come in well above consensus\\\"; \\\"a short seller accused the company of overstating receivables\\n  by $2 billion\\\". (4) A policy or regulatory decision or\\n  step, including one still in force that the item reports: \\\"the regulator approved the drug for a second indication\\\"; \\\"the\\n  government required licences for exports of the product\\\"; \\\"the regulator still bars sales of the drug in the country\\\".\\n- Go through the item from its first paragraph to its last, including paragraphs about matters other than the headline. The four\\n  kinds describe what material statements usually look like; they do not decide what is kept. Take a statement when an analyst\\n  following the company would write it in this week's notes because it makes some question's answer more or less likely, and no row\\n  of the closing check at the end of this request rejects it; a row's \\\"it does not cover\\\" clause sends the statement back to that test\\n  and keeps it only where the row says so. Most of an item is background, but a page with eight statements that pass yields eight\\n  evidence items, not a selection.\\n\\n2. LINK EACH STATEMENT TO ITS THESES\\n\\n- EXISTING: if an open thesis covers the statement, link it to that thesis's exact supplied ID, including contrary facts. Do not\\n  return or rename the thesis's description.\\n- NEW: if the statement raises a research question that merits separate tracking and no open or newly proposed thesis covers it,\\n  propose a thesis (section 3) and link the statement to it.\\n- NO THESIS YET: if no open or newly proposed thesis covers a statement you judge material, and the matter is not big enough by\\n  its own scale to merit a thesis (section 3), return it with empty links; it is kept without links.\\n- CONDITION: a statement of the company's own financial figures (a reported result or balance, its guidance, or a named party's\\n  or consensus estimate) lists its useful figures in figures. Its results, guidance and estimates, margins included, inform an open thesis about its revenue or earnings\\n  path when they bear on that path; a different period is not on its own a reason to leave them unlinked. When no open or new\\n  thesis covers it, its links stay empty. A figure is useful when it can change what an analyst\\n  expects of the company's revenue, profitability, funding or credit risk, or competitive position: a retailer's quarterly\\n  same-store sales, an airline's guidance for next year's unit costs and analysts' consensus earnings for a bank are; a cash-\nRESULT {\"new_theses\": [], \"evidence\": [], \"unresolved_links\": [], \"figure_issues\": [], \"unfiled_evidence\": [], \"unlinked_new_thesis_ids\": [], \"new_thesis_ids\": {}}\nSTATE []\nSEQUENCE 4 ID gnews:5594f685fcb554db910e45f8\nREQUEST {\"model\": \"deepseek-flash\", \"messages\": [{\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"ROLE\\n\\nYou are a senior hedge-fund analyst reading one source item for the company in scope (current_entity); \\\"the company\\\" below is that\\ncompany. The research already tracked for it is listed in open_theses. Your first job is evidence: decide by the rules below which\\nstatements in the item are material, then link each one to every thesis it informs: an open thesis that covers it, a new thesis you\\npropose, or both. Your second job is new theses: the important research questions this item raises that no open thesis\\ncovers. Every new thesis comes with evidence from this item. A material statement that no open thesis covers and that is not\\nitself a big matter (section 3) is returned with empty links: code keeps it in the company's record without links and links it\\nwhen a thesis that covers it opens. Never open a thesis only to give a statement a home.\\n\\nINPUT\\n\\n- current_entity and company_tags: the company in scope and the companies the item is tagged with. Code assigns the entity; do not\\n  invent one. Evidence can describe another party when it informs research on the company.\\n- open_theses: the identity, question, tracked outcome and period of each thesis already tracked. They tell you where evidence belongs\\n  and whether an idea is already covered; they are not evidence. You do not see the evidence already stored; a later step compares\\n  new evidence with history.\\n- source_item: the complete text and its source metadata.\\n\\n1. EVIDENCE: WHAT TO KEEP\\n\\n- A material statement is one of four kinds. (1) The company's own figure, guidance or decision: \\\"same-store sales rose 4.2% in the\\n  quarter\\\"; \\\"management guided full-year revenue to $12\\u201313 billion\\\"; \\\"the board approved a $10 billion buyback\\\". (2) A customer's,\\n  supplier's, competitor's or partner's action or reported figure with a size, date or name that bears on the company's demand,\\n  supply, input costs, competition, prices or products: \\\"the largest customer said it will cut orders by a fifth next year\\\"; \\\"a bank\\n  chose a rival's system\\\"; \\\"a software firm is integrating the company's model into its government systems\\\"; \\\"the main supplier said\\n  its capacity will double by 2027\\\"; \\\"a rival cut list prices 15% in Europe\\\"; \\\"the largest customer reported its order backlog doubled\\n  to $40 billion\\\". An action is a step the item reports as news: taken, decided or planned (an order, a contract, a selection, an\\n  integration under way, an expansion, a cut, a price change, a deployment plan). A party that already uses, hosts, runs or has built\\n  on the company's products, and the benefits it describes from that use (faster, cheaper, more accurate), describe an arrangement in\\n  place, which is background; its own revenue, capacity, orders or contracted amounts are figures under (2) (\\\"a hospital chain uses\\n  the company's scheduling software and reports shorter waits\\\"). (3) A named party's new claim or estimate about the company or its\\n  industry, with or without a figure, whether an analyst, a customer's or rival's executive or an official makes it: \\\"the CFO said\\n  plants built a decade ago still run at full capacity\\\"; \\\"an analyst raised next year's EPS estimate to $4.10\\\"; \\\"an analyst said the\\n  bank's loan losses next quarter will come in well above consensus\\\"; \\\"a short seller accused the company of overstating receivables\\n  by $2 billion\\\". (4) A policy or regulatory decision or\\n  step, including one still in force that the item reports: \\\"the regulator approved the drug for a second indication\\\"; \\\"the\\n  government required licences for exports of the product\\\"; \\\"the regulator still bars sales of the drug in the country\\\".\\n- Go through the item from its first paragraph to its last, including paragraphs about matters other than the headline. The four\\n  kinds describe what material statements usually look like; they do not decide what is kept. Take a statement when an analyst\\n  following the company would write it in this week's notes because it makes some question's answer more or less likely, and no row\\n  of the closing check at the end of this request rejects it; a row's \\\"it does not cover\\\" clause sends the statement back to that test\\n  and keeps it only where the row says so. Most of an item is background, but a page with eight statements that pass yields eight\\n  evidence items, not a selection.\\n\\n2. LINK EACH STATEMENT TO ITS THESES\\n\\n- EXISTING: if an open thesis covers the statement, link it to that thesis's exact supplied ID, including contrary facts. Do not\\n  return or rename the thesis's description.\\n- NEW: if the statement raises a research question that merits separate tracking and no open or newly proposed thesis covers it,\\n  propose a thesis (section 3) and link the statement to it.\\n- NO THESIS YET: if no open or newly proposed thesis covers a statement you judge material, and the matter is not big enough by\\n  its own scale to merit a thesis (section 3), return it with empty links; it is kept without links.\\n- CONDITION: a statement of the company's own financial figures (a reported result or balance, its guidance, or a named party's\\n  or consensus estimate) lists its useful figures in figures. Its results, guidance and estimates, margins included, inform an open thesis about its revenue or earnings\\n  path when they bear on that path; a different period is not on its own a reason to leave them unlinked. When no open or new\\n  thesis covers it, its links stay empty. A figure is useful when it can change what an analyst\\n  expects of the company's revenue, profitability, funding or credit risk, or competitive position: a retailer's quarterly\\n  same-store sales, an airline's guidance for next year's unit costs and analysts' consensus earnings for a bank are; a cash-rich\\n  company's cash balance or cash flow usually changes none of these when the item ties it to nothing, while a heavily indebted\\n  firm's cash burn changes its credit risk. The share price, a target price and other companies' figures are never conditions.\\n- Link one statement to every thesis it informs, and to none that its own relevance says it does not inform; use the narrowest\\n  relevant thesis only when choosing between theses that cover the same thing.\\n- Match by the underlying question or outcome, subject and scope, and time when relevant, not by word overlap. A different wording,\\n  source, forecast or direction alone does not make a new thesis. Related questions can be separate when their outcomes matter\\n  independently.\\n- Do not exclude evidence solely because it concerns a related period, population or product variant. Assess whether it can inform\\n  the open question, preserving the difference in the record. Do not present a forecast for a later year as a forecast for the\\n  thesis's year. If another open thesis covers the evidence more directly, consider that thesis as well; do not assume different\\n  periods are automatically the same thesis or automatically unrelated.\\n\\nExamples of linking evidence to research:\\n\\n- Supply chain: an open thesis asks whether a manufacturer's production is constrained. A named supplier announces a capacity\\n  expansion for a component the manufacturer uses. Link the announcement as a potential supply signal; it does not establish\\n  allocations or deliveries.\\n- Geopolitics: an open thesis asks whether an exporter can sell in a specified market. The relevant government proposes a licensing\\n  restriction on that product. Link the proposal as evidence about market access; a proposal is not an enacted restriction.\\n- Macro: an open thesis asks whether a company's borrowing costs will rise. The item reports that its floating-rate debt is tied to a\\n  benchmark that has risen. Link the benchmark movement and the stated exposure; do not calculate the expense change unless the\\n  source supports it.\\n- Competition: an open thesis asks whether a company can retain customers in a product market. A named competitor cuts the price of a\\n  competing product. Link the price cut as competitive evidence; do not assert lost customers or share without support.\\n\\n3. WHEN TO PROPOSE A NEW THESIS\\n\\nA thesis is a question a senior analyst would hold a card for: its answer could change a position because it could change the\\ncompany's revenue, profit, cash or credit risk, or competitive position in a way you can name; public material will settle it on a\\ndate; and no open thesis already asks it. A thesis asks one quantity for one period, or one event. It opens for one of four reasons, a closed set; a matter that fits none of them is\\nevidence, kept and linked to the theses it informs or left unlinked, never a thesis:\\n- product_node: when the generation of the main product that carries the company's value reaches the step that brings revenue,\\n  usually first shipments; announcements and samples are its evidence, and only milestones an analyst bets on separately (a drug's\\n  trial result and its approval) are separate theses (\\\"will the carmaker's new platform start deliveries in 2026?\\\";\\n  \\\"will the drugmaker's next-generation inhaler, the product line that carries its value, reach pharmacies by mid-2027?\\\").\\n- binding_obligation: an obligation the company takes on in its own name, large enough to change its revenue, profit, cash or credit\\n  risk: guaranteeing, backing or giving credit support for another party's debt, lease, revenue or capacity, lending to it,\\n  committing to buy capacity or services, or a sized stake or acquisition not yet completed. One already undertaken is tracked by its\\n  amount at the next annual report; one not yet signed by whether it is signed by a check date within 18 months of the item (\\\"how much will the\\n  utility have guaranteed for its project companies' loans at its next annual report?\\\"; \\\"will the retailer sign a guarantee for its\\n  franchisees' leases, large enough to change its credit risk, by the check date?\\\"). Its own borrowing or leases and a completed\\n  or unsized investment are not obligations.\\n- shared_quantity: a quantity or price outside any one company that several companies depend on, for this or next calendar year:\\n  several companies' combined output or spending, a contract price, an interest rate (\\\"the largest container lines' combined capacity\\n  added in 2026\\\"; \\\"next year's average contract price of a raw material\\\"). Another population or wording of the same aggregate is\\n  evidence for it.\\n- policy_stage: the step a policy or regulatory process reaches, naming who decides (\\\"will the regulator approve the drug for a second\\n  indication?\\\"; \\\"will the government require licences for the product's exports by a date?\\\").\\n\\n- Open only matters an analyst would bet on separately; small matters open no thesis and their statements are kept as evidence. A\\n  customer's deployment plan is evidence for the company's demand and product theses, unless it changes the company's own cash or\\n  credit risk, which is judged as an obligation.\\n- Give the date the item supports. When it gives none, use the date the reason fixes and say how it was set: an obligation already\\n  undertaken, its next annual report; an unsigned obligation, a check date within 18 months of the item; a pending regulatory\\n  decision, the current fiscal year-end. A shared quantity takes its release date and a product event the date its outcome can be\\n  checked, only when the item supports one. A question no date can settle is rewritten or not opened.\\n- Do not open a thesis about: an executive appointment or organisational change; the company's own borrowing or leases as an\\n  obligation; a buyback authorisation or dividend declaration; a financial measure's level, growth, margin or guidance for any period,\\n  on its own; an undated moat or strategy; a feature, content or software update, price point, regional service or variant of a\\n  product already on sale; another estimate, source or wording of what an open thesis tracks; an annual quantity after next year (an\\n  obligation excepted); shareholder votes, bylaws or routine litigation procedure. Their statements are evidence for the theses they\\n  inform, or left unlinked.\\n- Before proposing a new thesis, name its reason (opens_as), the public material that will settle it (settled_by), and the closest\\n  open thesis with why an analyst would bet on the two separately because their answers can move apart; with no open thesis, say so.\\n  If the two cannot move apart, link the evidence to every open thesis it informs instead.\\n\\nCounterexamples:\\n\\n- \\\"The company's outlook is interesting\\\" or \\\"the industry may change\\\" is too vague: it names no outcome later evidence can inform.\\n- If an open thesis tracks sales growth, a revised sales forecast is evidence for it; do not open a second thesis to repeat it.\\n- A customer expanding capacity informs demand; it is not a thesis of its own, and not a claim that the supplier has received orders.\\n- \\\"Is the stock attractively valued?\\\" is an investment conclusion, not a research question about the business.\\n\\n4. HOW TO WRITE EVIDENCE\\n\\n- One evidence item records one statement, and that statement is what you judge: a figure, date or comparison in the neighbouring\\n  words does not make another claim in the same passage new or material. A statement told in several sentences of the item, with its\\n  terms, figures and conditions, is one item.\\n- Write a standalone summary: who did or said what, how much and for which period, naming the measure even when a neighbouring\\n  sentence gives it. Preserve conditions, uncertainty and attribution. Return every passage the summary rests on, each copied\\n  verbatim and contiguously from the item, without translation; do not join distant passages with an ellipsis.\\n- Name the actor in the summary and identify the original party whose action, figure or claim it is. Give the reporting speaker when\\n  available. The item's publisher and publication date come from its source metadata.\\n- Use only this item's information. Distinguish forecasts, allegations and unconfirmed reports from confirmed outcomes. Report plans\\n  as plans; do not infer committed funding, orders, deliveries or revenue.\\n- For each item, return its evidence tier, the original statement date and the period the statement describes. These are separate\\n  pieces of information.\\n\\nEVIDENCE TIER\\n\\nClassify the statement by what is confirmed, not how important it is:\\n\\n- rumour: the item attributes the claim to unnamed sources or calls it unconfirmed.\\n- fact: an official action, published actual figure or completed transaction, including an announcement or completed transaction\\n  reported by an article.\\n- party_statement: a company's guidance, management statement or official's remarks. A forecast is a statement, not a completed\\n  outcome.\\n- third_party_view: an analyst's, journalist's or expert's opinion or estimate.\\n\\nApply the first matching category in that order. An unnamed-source or unconfirmed claim remains a rumour even if it describes an\\naction or number. If none matches and the claim is unattributed, use rumour. The tier is not an importance score.\\n\\nEVIDENCE TIME\\n\\n- statement_date: the original statement's full day in YYYY-MM-DD when known; otherwise null. Do not use the article date as the\\n  original date unless the statement was made in the originating party's own release, filing, call or analysis. Do not assign that\\n  date to an older statement quoted there.\\n- statement_date_text: the original date or partial date as stated, if present; otherwise null. Preserve a month or relative date\\n  without inventing a day. A forecast horizon or an occasion is not a date.\\n- evidence_period: the period the reported fact, plan or forecast covers, as supported by the item; otherwise null. This is not the\\n  statement date.\\n\\nReturn the theses an\nRAW {\"id\": \"46e56ac5-5d4b-477d-afad-0f193b66e3e9\", \"object\": \"chat.completion\", \"created\": 1794688529, \"model\": \"deepseek-flash\", \"choices\": [{\"index\": 0, \"message\": {\"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": \"{\\n  \\\"new_theses\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"local_id\\\": \\\"T1\\\",\\n      \\\"description\\\": \\\"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\\\",\\n      \\\"outcome\\\": \\\"The disclosed amount of guarantees, credit-support commitments and revenue backstops NVIDIA has extended to AI labs and cloud partners (including the credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity for one additional AI lab), versus the equity investments it has made, at NVIDIA's next annual report.\\\",\\n      \\\"period\\\": \\\"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\\\",\\n      \\\"opens_as\\\": \\\"binding_obligation\\\",\\n      \\\"settled_by\\\": \\\"NVIDIA's Form 10-K for fiscal 2027 (guarantees, commitments and contingencies note) and any 8-K or earnings disclosure quantifying the credit support for the additional AI lab's ~2 GW of computing capacity and the minimum-revenue guarantees to cloud partners.\\\",\\n      \\\"closest_open_thesis\\\": null,\\n      \\\"why_a_separate_bet\\\": \\\"The open thesis list is empty. The question is separate from any revenue-growth or demand question because the guarantee/credit-support total can expand or contract, and change NVIDIA's credit risk and its exposure to circular financing, independently of whether reported revenue growth holds up.\\\"\\n    }\\n  ],\\n  \\\"evidence\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"local_id\\\": \\\"E1\\\",\\n      \\\"summary\\\": \\\"NVIDIA management expects roughly a quarter (about 25%) of the company's fiscal 2028 business to come from AI labs that NVIDIA will back with its own balance sheet.\\\",\\n      \\\"source_passages\\\": [\\n        \\\"If you hold NVIDIA (NVDA) stock, one number deserves your attention: roughly a quarter. That is the share of fiscal 2028 business management expects from AI labs it will back with its own balance sheet.\\\",\\n        \\\"Roughly a quarter of expected fiscal 2028 business comes from AI labs NVIDIA plans to back.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"original_party\\\": \\\"NVIDIA management\\\",\\n      \\\"reporting_speaker\\\": \\\"Trefis\\\",\\n      \\\"tier\\\": \\\"party_statement\\\",\\n      \\\"statement_date\\\": null,\\n      \\\"statement_date_text\\\": null,\\n      \\\"evidence_period\\\": \\\"Fiscal 2028\\\",\\n      \\\"links\\\": [\\n        {\\n          \\\"target_type\\\": \\\"new\\\",\\n          \\\"target_id\\\": \\\"T1\\\",\\n          \\\"relevance\\\": \\\"Management's expectation that about a quarter of fiscal 2028 business comes from labs NVIDIA backs sizes the revenue dependence that makes the scale of its credit support and guarantees consequential for credit and earnings risk; it does not itself quantify the obligations.\\\"\\n        }\\n      ],\\n      \\\"figures\\\": [\\n        {\\n          \\\"measure\\\": \\\"Share of fiscal 2028 business expected from AI labs NVIDIA will back with its own balance sheet\\\",\\n          \\\"period\\\": \\\"Fiscal 2028\\\",\\n          \\\"value\\\": \\\"roughly a quarter\\\",\\n          \\\"kind\\\": \\\"guidance\\\",\\n          \\\"basis\\\": \\\"Management expectation, company-level business share\\\"\\n        }\\n      ]\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"local_id\\\": \\\"E2\\\",\\n      \\\"summary\\\": \\\"NVIDIA's revenue more than doubled year over year in fiscal Q2 2027.\\\",\\n      \\\"source_passages\\\": [\\n        \\\"NVIDIA’s revenue more than doubled year over year in fiscal Q2 2027, so this number is easy to miss.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"original_party\\\": \\\"NVIDIA\\\",\\n      \\\"reporting_speaker\\\": \\\"Trefis\\\",\\n      \\\"tier\\\": \\\"fact\\\",\\n      \\\"statement_date\\\": null,\\n      \\\"statement_date_text\\\": null,\\n      \\\"evidence_period\\\": \\\"Fiscal Q2 2027\\\",\\n      \\\"links\\\": [],\\n      \\\"figures\\\": [\\n        {\\n          \\\"measure\\\": \\\"Revenue, year-over-year change\\\",\\n          \\\"period\\\": \\\"Fiscal Q2 2027\\\",\\n          \\\"value\\\": \\\"more than doubled\\\",\\n          \\\"kind\\\": \\\"actual\\\",\\n          \\\"basis\\\": \\\"Company-level, year over year\\\"\\n        }\\n      ]\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"local_id\\\": \\\"E3\\\",\\n      \\\"summary\\\": \\\"According to management, NVIDIA has already invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs.\\\",\\n      \\\"source_passages\\\": [\\n        \\\"NVIDIA has already invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs, according to management.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"original_party\\\": \\\"NVIDIA management\\\",\\n      \\\"reporting_speaker\\\": \\\"Trefis\\\",\\n      \\\"tier\\\": \\\"party_statement\\\",\\n      \\\"statement_date\\\": null,\\n      \\\"statement_date_text\\\": null,\\n      \\\"evidence_period\\\": null,\\n      \\\"links\\\": [],\\n      \\\"figures\\\": [\\n        {\\n          \\\"measure\\\": \\\"Investments in frontier AI labs\\\",\\n          \\\"period\\\": null,\\n          \\\"value\\\": \\\"nearly $50 billion\\\",\\n          \\\"kind\\\": \\\"actual\\\",\\n          \\\"basis\\\": \\\"Cumulative, according to management\\\"\\n        }\\n      ]\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"local_id\\\": \\\"E4\\\",\\n      \\\"summary\\\": \\\"Business Insider reported that NVIDIA's overall equity portfolio has reached $99 billion, up from roughly $7 billion in summer 2025 — a 14-fold increase.\\\",\\n      \\\"source_passages\\\": [\\n        \\\"Those lab investments are part of a broader expansion: Business Insider reported that NVIDIA’s overall equity portfolio has reached $99 billion. In summer 2025, that portfolio was roughly $7 billion. That is a 14-fold increase, so this is not a mild change.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"original_party\\\": \\\"Business Insider\\\",\\n      \\\"reporting_speaker\\\": \\\"Trefis, citing Business Insider\\\",\\n      \\\"tier\\\": \\\"third_party_view\\\",\\n      \\\"statement_date\\\": null,\\n      \\\"statement_date_text\\\": null,\\n      \\\"evidence_period\\\": \\\"As of the report (2026) versus summer 2025\\\",\\n      \\\"links\\\": [],\\n      \\\"figures\\\": [\\n        {\\n          \\\"measure\\\": \\\"Overall equity portfolio value\\\",\\n          \\\"period\\\": null,\\n          \\\"value\\\": \\\"$99 billion\\\",\\n          \\\"kind\\\": \\\"estimate\\\",\\n          \\\"basis\\\": \\\"Business Insider report, overall equity portfolio\\\"\\n        },\\n        {\\n          \\\"measure\\\": \\\"Overall equity portfolio value\\\",\\n          \\\"period\\\": \\\"Summer 2025\\\",\\n          \\\"value\\\": \\\"roughly $7 billion\\\",\\n          \\\"kind\\\": \\\"estimate\\\",\\n          \\\"basis\\\": \\\"Business Insider report, overall equity portfolio\\\"\\n        }\\n      ]\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"local_id\\\": \\\"E5\\\",\\n      \\\"summary\\\": \\\"NVIDIA plans to provide credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity for one additional AI lab.\\\",\\n      \\\"source_passages\\\": [\\n        \\\"For one more AI lab, it will provide credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"original_party\\\": \\\"NVIDIA\\\",\\n      \\\"reporting_speaker\\\": \\\"Trefis\\\",\\n      \\\"tier\\\": \\\"party_statement\\\",\\n      \\\"statement_date\\\": null,\\n      \\\"statement_date_text\\\": null,\\n      \\\"evidence_period\\\": null,\\n      \\\"links\\\": [\\n        {\\n          \\\"target_type\\\": \\\"new\\\",\\n          \\\"target_id\\\": \\\"T1\\\",\\n          \\\"relevance\\\": \\\"A planned credit-support commitment for another party's computing capacity is the obligation the thesis tracks; the amount of the support is not quantified in dollars in the item.\\\"\\n        }\\n      ],\\n      \\\"figures\\\": []\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"local_id\\\": \\\"E6\\\",\\n      \\\"summary\\\": \\\"NVIDIA guarantees a minimum level of revenue on part of a facility's capacity with some cloud partners.\\\",\\n      \\\"source_passages\\\": [\\n        \\\"With some cloud partners, it guarantees a minimum level of revenue on part of a facility’s capacity.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"original_party\\\": \\\"NVIDIA\\\",\\n      \\\"reporting_speaker\\\": \\\"Trefis\\\",\\n      \\\"tier\\\": \\\"party_statement\\\",\\n      \\\"statement_date\\\": null,\\n      \\\"statement_date_text\\\": null,\\n      \\\"evidence_period\\\": null,\\n      \\\"links\\\": [\\n        {\\n          \\\"target_type\\\": \\\"new\\\",\\n          \\\"target_id\\\": \\\"T1\\\",\\n          \\\"relevance\\\": \\\"A minimum-revenue guarantee given to cloud partners is a credit-support obligation of the kind the thesis quantifies; the item gives no dollar amount.\\\"\\n        }\\n      ],\\n      \\\"figures\\\": []\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"local_id\\\": \\\"E7\\\",\\n      \\\"summary\\\": \\\"NVIDIA's days of sales outstanding, the average time to collect cash after a sale, increased to 60 days; management said this reflects extended payment terms for large purchases by certain investment-grade customers, shipped over multiple quarters.\\\",\\n      \\\"source_passages\\\": [\\n        \\\"There is also a change in how quickly NVIDIA gets paid. Days of sales outstanding, the average time to collect cash after a sale, increased to 60 days. Management said this reflects extended payment terms for large purchases by certain investment-grade customers, shipped over multiple quarters.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"original_party\\\": \\\"NVIDIA\\\",\\n      \\\"reporting_speaker\\\": \\\"Trefis, citing NVIDIA management\\\",\\n      \\\"tier\\\": \\\"fact\\\",\\n      \\\"statement_date\\\": null,\\n      \\\"statement_date_text\\\": null,\\n      \\\"evidence_period\\\": null,\\n      \\\"links\\\": [],\\n      \\\"figures\\\": [\\n        {\\n          \\\"measure\\\": \\\"Days of sales outstanding\\\",\\n          \\\"period\\\": null,\\n          \\\"value\\\": \\\"60 days\\\",\\n          \\\"kind\\\": \\\"actual\\\",\\n          \\\"basis\\\": \\\"Average time to collect cash after a sale; management attributes the increase to extended payment terms for large purchases by certain investment-grade customers shipped over multiple quarters\\\"\\n        }\\n      ]\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"local_id\\\": \\\"E8\\\",\\n      \\\"summary\\\": \\\"OpenAI's existing and planned commitments represent about 12 gigawatts of NVIDIA computing, and OpenAI has committed to deployments through 2030.\\\",\\n      \\\"source_passages\\\": [\\n        \\\"OpenAI’s existing and planned commitments represent about 12 gigawatts of NVIDIA computing. OpenAI has committed to deployments through 2030.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"original_party\\\": \\\"OpenAI\\\",\\n      \\\"reporting_speaker\\\": \\\"Trefis, citing NVIDIA management\\\",\\n      \\\"tier\\\": \\\"fact\\\",\\n      \\\"statement_date\\\": null,\\n      \\\"statement_date_text\\\": null,\\n      \\\"evidence_period\\\": \\\"Existing and planned commitments; deployments through 2030\\\",\\n      \\\"links\\\": [],\\n      \\\"figures\\\": []\\n    }\\n  ]\\n}\", \"reasoning_content\": \"Let me analyze this item carefully.\\n\\nThe source is a Trefis analyst article dated 2026-09-22 about NVIDIA. Open theses: none.\\n\\nSo every material statement either opens a new thesis (if it fits one of four reasons) or is kept unlinked.\\n\\nLet me go through the statements.\\n\\n1. \\\"roughly a quarter. That is the share of fiscal 2028 business management expects from AI labs it will back with its own balance sheet.\\\" — This is a company forecast/guidance statement: management expects ~25% of fiscal 2028 business from AI labs it will back. Material. Figures: share of fiscal 2028 business from backed AI labs (~25%). It's an estimate/guidance. Kind: guidance (management's own outlook). Period: fiscal 2028.\\n\\nDoes it open a new thesis? It's about the company's revenue mix and dependence on backed customers — arguably a financial measure/guidance, which section 3 says not to open a thesis about (\\\"a financial measure's level, growth, margin or guidance for any period, on its own\\\"). It's not a binding obligation per se. So keep as evidence with empty links? Hmm, but there is a possible thesis about circular financing / credit support to AI labs — the credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity could be a binding_obligation: NVIDIA providing credit support for another party's capacity is a binding obligation the company takes on in its own name, large enough to change its credit risk. That's exactly the binding_obligation reason: \\\"guaranteeing, backing or giving credit support for another party's debt, lease, revenue or capacity.\\\"\\n\\nSo a new thesis: \\\"How much credit support / guarantees will NVIDIA have extended to AI labs and cloud partners at its next annual report?\\\" opens_as binding_obligation. Settled by: NVIDIA's annual report (fiscal 2028? or next 10-K) disclosing guarantees/credit support. Period: next annual report date. Item date 2026-09-22; NVIDIA fiscal year ends late January. Next annual report would be ~February 2027 for FY2027. The item mentions fiscal Q2 2027 call and fiscal Q3 2027 earnings call Nov 17 (2026). So next annual report: fiscal 2027 10-K, around Feb 2027.\\n\\nAlso the guarantee of minimum revenue to cloud partners: \\\"With some cloud partners, it guarantees a minimum level of revenue on part of a facility's capacity.\\\" That's a binding obligation too.\\n\\n2. \\\"NVIDIA has already invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs, according to management.\\\" — a completed investment. Completed investment is not an obligation (\\\"a completed or unsized investment are not obligations\\\"). So it's evidence, linked maybe to the same new thesis? A completed investment isn't an obligation, but the new thesis about credit support — the $50B investment is related but different. Hmm. Could the thesis be broader: \\\"how much will NVIDIA's credit support and guarantees to customers amount to\\\"? The investment isn't credit support. Let me consider: keep the $50B investment as evidence. Link to the binding obligation thesis? Relevance: it shows scale of backing but is equity investment, not obligation. I think linking is defensible as context, but rules say link to theses it informs. The thesis asks about guarantees/credit support amounts; equity investments aren't that. But could be a separate consideration. Hmm.\\n\\n3. \\\"Business Insider reported that NVIDIA's overall equity portfolio has reached $99 billion. In summer 2025, that portfolio was roughly $7 billion. That is a 14-fold increase\\\" — a figure from Business Insider report, third_party? Actually it's a reported figure of NVIDIA's portfolio, reported by Business Insider — tier: fact? The portfolio value is a reported actual figure. Original party: Business Insider (reporting outlet making the estimate/report). Tier: third_party_view? It's a reported figure by another outlet—the claim is a fact-like report but not officially disclosed. I'd say third_party_view (Business Insider's report/estimate). Hmm, evidence tier: \\\"fact: an official action, published actual figure or completed transaction, including an announcement or completed transaction reported by an article.\\\" A reported portfolio value of $99B is a reported actual figure reported by an article. But it's not official. I'll call it third_party_view since Business Insider is the source of the figure. Actually, the values of equity holdings can be computed from filings; Business Insider reporting a figure. I'd lean third_party_view.\\n\\n4. \\\"Days of sales outstanding ... increased to 60 days. Management said this reflects extended payment terms for large purchases by certain investment-grade customers, shipped over multiple quarters.\\\" — material: change in collection times. DSO rising to 60 days — a company financial/credit metric. This bears on credit risk and funding. Is it \\\"financial measure's level\\\" on its own -> no thesis. Keep as evidence, empty links. Figures: DSO 60 days.\\n\\n5. \\\"Management said in the fiscal Q2 2027 call that some will call NVIDIA's support circular financing.\\\" — characterisation, not a new fact. Also \\\"Management says a surge in AI demand is driving a global buildout\\\" — general. \\\"revenue-sharing deals with cloud partners\\\" — arrangement in place; describes. \\\"These deals can expand addressable market, create recurring revenue\\\" — vague outlook. These probably don't pass.\\n\\n6. \\\"For one more AI lab, it will provide credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity\nRESULT {\"new_theses\": [{\"local_id\": \"T1\", \"description\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\", \"outcome\": \"The disclosed amount of guarantees, credit-support commitments and revenue backstops NVIDIA has extended to AI labs and cloud partners (including the credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity for one additional AI lab), versus the equity investments it has made, at NVIDIA's next annual report.\", \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\", \"opens_as\": \"binding_obligation\", \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA's Form 10-K for fiscal 2027 (guarantees, commitments and contingencies note) and any 8-K or earnings disclosure quantifying the credit support for the additional AI lab's ~2 GW of computing capacity and the minimum-revenue guarantees to cloud partners.\", \"closest_open_thesis\": null, \"why_a_separate_bet\": \"The open thesis list is empty. The question is separate from any revenue-growth or demand question because the guarantee/credit-support total can expand or contract, and change NVIDIA's credit risk and its exposure to circular financing, independently of whether reported revenue growth holds up.\"}], \"evidence\": [{\"local_id\": \"E1\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management expects roughly a quarter (about 25%) of the company's fiscal 2028 business to come from AI labs that NVIDIA will back with its own balance sheet.\", \"source_passages\": [\"If you hold NVIDIA (NVDA) stock, one number deserves your attention: roughly a quarter. That is the share of fiscal 2028 business management expects from AI labs it will back with its own balance sheet.\", \"Roughly a quarter of expected fiscal 2028 business comes from AI labs NVIDIA plans to back.\"], \"original_party\": \"NVIDIA management\", \"reporting_speaker\": \"Trefis\", \"tier\": \"party_statement\", \"statement_date\": null, \"statement_date_text\": null, \"evidence_period\": \"Fiscal 2028\", \"links\": [{\"target_type\": \"new\", \"target_id\": \"T1\", \"relevance\": \"Management's expectation that about a quarter of fiscal 2028 business comes from labs NVIDIA backs sizes the revenue dependence that makes the scale of its credit support and guarantees consequential for credit and earnings risk; it does not itself quantify the obligations.\"}], \"figures\": [{\"measure\": \"Share of fiscal 2028 business expected from AI labs NVIDIA will back with its own balance sheet\", \"period\": \"Fiscal 2028\", \"value\": \"roughly a quarter\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": \"Management expectation, company-level business share\"}], \"verified_source_passages\": [\"If you hold NVIDIA (NVDA) stock, one number deserves your attention: roughly a quarter. That is the share of fiscal 2028 business management expects from AI labs it will back with its own balance sheet.\", \"Roughly a quarter of expected fiscal 2028 business comes from AI labs NVIDIA plans to back.\"], \"source_passages_repaired\": [false, false]}, {\"local_id\": \"E2\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's revenue more than doubled year over year in fiscal Q2 2027.\", \"source_passages\": [\"NVIDIA’s revenue more than doubled year over year in fiscal Q2 2027, so this number is easy to miss.\"], \"original_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"reporting_speaker\": \"Trefis\", \"tier\": \"fact\", \"statement_date\": null, \"statement_date_text\": null, \"evidence_period\": \"Fiscal Q2 2027\", \"links\": [], \"figures\": [{\"measure\": \"Revenue, year-over-year change\", \"period\": \"Fiscal Q2 2027\", \"value\": \"more than doubled\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"Company-level, year over year\"}], \"verified_source_passages\": [\"NVIDIA’s revenue more than doubled year over year in fiscal Q2 2027, so this number is easy to miss.\"], \"source_passages_repaired\": [false]}, {\"local_id\": \"E3\", \"summary\": \"According to management, NVIDIA has already invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs.\", \"source_passages\": [\"NVIDIA has already invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs, according to management.\"], \"original_party\": \"NVIDIA management\", \"reporting_speaker\": \"Trefis\", \"tier\": \"party_statement\", \"statement_date\": null, \"statement_date_text\": null, \"evidence_period\": null, \"links\": [], \"figures\": [{\"measure\": \"Investments in frontier AI labs\", \"period\": null, \"value\": \"nearly $50 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"Cumulative, according to management\"}], \"verified_source_passages\": [\"NVIDIA has already invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs, according to management.\"], \"source_passages_repaired\": [false]}, {\"local_id\": \"E4\", \"summary\": \"Business Insider reported that NVIDIA's overall equity portfolio has reached $99 billion, up from roughly $7 billion in summer 2025 — a 14-fold increase.\", \"source_passages\": [\"Those lab investments are part of a broader expansion: Business Insider reported that NVIDIA’s overall equity portfolio has reached $99 billion. In summer 2025, that portfolio was roughly $7 billion. That is a 14-fold increase, so this is not a mild change.\"], \"original_party\": \"Business Insider\", \"reporting_speaker\": \"Trefis, citing Business Insider\", \"tier\": \"third_party_view\", \"statement_date\": null, \"statement_date_text\": null, \"evidence_period\": \"As of the report (2026) versus summer 2025\", \"links\": [], \"figures\": [{\"measure\": \"Overall equity portfolio value\", \"period\": null, \"value\": \"$99 billion\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"Business Insider report, overall equity portfolio\"}, {\"measure\": \"Overall equity portfolio value\", \"period\": \"Summer 2025\", \"value\": \"roughly $7 billion\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"Business Insider report, overall equity portfolio\"}], \"verified_source_passages\": [\"Those lab investments are part of a broader expansion: Business Insider reported that NVIDIA’s overall equity portfolio has reached $99 billion. In summer 2025, that portfolio was roughly $7 billion. That is a 14-fold increase, so this is not a mild change.\"], \"source_passages_repaired\": [false]}, {\"local_id\": \"E5\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA plans to provide credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity for one additional AI lab.\", \"source_passages\": [\"For one more AI lab, it will provide credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity.\"], \"original_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"reporting_speaker\": \"Trefis\", \"tier\": \"party_statement\", \"statement_date\": null, \"statement_date_text\": null, \"evidence_period\": null, \"links\": [{\"target_type\": \"new\", \"target_id\": \"T1\", \"relevance\": \"A planned credit-support commitment for another party's computing capacity is the obligation the thesis tracks; the amount of the support is not quantified in dollars in the item.\"}], \"figures\": [], \"verified_source_passages\": [\"For one more AI lab, it will provide credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity.\"], \"source_passages_repaired\": [false]}, {\"local_id\": \"E6\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA guarantees a minimum level of revenue on part of a facility's capacity with some cloud partners.\", \"source_passages\": [\"With some cloud partners, it guarantees a minimum level of revenue on part of a facility’s capacity.\"], \"original_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"reporting_speaker\": \"Trefis\", \"tier\": \"party_statement\", \"statement_date\": null, \"statement_date_text\": null, \"evidence_period\": null, \"links\": [{\"target_type\": \"new\", \"target_id\": \"T1\", \"relevance\": \"A minimum-revenue guarantee given to cloud partners is a credit-support obligation of the kind the thesis quantifies; the item gives no dollar amount.\"}], \"figures\": [], \"verified_source_passages\": [\"With some cloud partners, it guarantees a minimum level of revenue on part of a facility’s capacity.\"], \"source_passages_repaired\": [false]}, {\"local_id\": \"E7\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's days of sales outstanding, the average time to collect cash after a sale, increased to 60 days; management said this reflects extended payment terms for large purchases by certain investment-grade customers, shipped over multiple quarters.\", \"source_passages\": [\"There is also a change in how quickly NVIDIA gets paid. Days of sales outstanding, the average time to collect cash after a sale, increased to 60 days. Management said this reflects extended payment terms for large purchases by certain investment-grade customers, shipped over multiple quarters.\"], \"original_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"reporting_speaker\": \"Trefis, citing NVIDIA management\", \"tier\": \"fact\", \"statement_date\": null, \"statement_date_text\": null, \"evidence_period\": null, \"links\": [], \"figures\": [{\"measure\": \"Days of sales outstanding\", \"period\": null, \"value\": \"60 days\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"Average time to collect cash after a sale; management attributes the increase to extended payment terms for large purchases by certain investment-grade customers shipped over multiple quarters\"}], \"verified_source_passages\": [\"There is also a change in how quickly NVIDIA gets paid. Days of sales outstanding, the average time to collect cash after a sale, increased to 60 days. Management said this reflects extended payment terms for large purchases by certain investment-grade customers, shipped over multiple quarters.\"], \"source_passages_repaired\": [false]}, {\"local_id\": \"E8\", \"summary\": \"OpenAI's existing and planned commitments represent about 12 gigawatts of NVIDIA computing, and OpenAI has committed to deployments through 2030.\", \"source_passages\": [\"OpenAI’s existing and planned commitments represent about 12 gigawatts of NVIDIA computing. OpenAI has committed to deployments through 2030.\"], \"original_party\": \"OpenAI\", \"reporting_speaker\": \"Trefis, citing NVIDIA management\", \"tier\": \"fact\", \"statement_date\": null, \"statement_date_text\": null, \"evidence_period\": \"Existing and planned commitments; deployments through 2030\", \"links\": [], \"figures\": [], \"verified_source_passages\": [\"OpenAI’s existing and planned commitments represent about 12 gigawatts of NVIDIA computing. OpenAI has committed to deployments through 2030.\"], \"source_passages_repaired\": [false]}], \"unresolved_links\": [], \"figure_issues\": [], \"unfiled_evidence\": [], \"unlinked_new_thesis_ids\": [], \"new_thesis_ids\": {\"T1\": \"clean-1\"}}\nSTATE [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"entity\": \"NVDA\", \"description\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\", \"outcome\": \"The disclosed amount of guarantees, credit-support commitments and revenue backstops NVIDIA has extended to AI labs and cloud partners (including the credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity for one additional AI lab), versus the equity investments it has made, at NVIDIA's next annual report.\", \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"}]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_31","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import sqlite3,json; c=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:poc/data/clean-lean-week-2026-11-14/week-r2/c1/step1/trace.sqlite?mode=ro\\\",uri=True); rows=c.execute(\\\"select sequence,input_id,raw_response,result from trace where result like ?\\\",(\\\"%45%\\\",));\nfor n,i,raw,res in rows:\n d=json.loads(res); ev=[e for e in d.get(\\\"evidence\\\",[]) if \\\"70%\\\" in str(e) and \\\"45%\\\" in str(e)];\n if ev:\n  print(\\\"STEP\\\",n,\\\"ID\\\",i);print(\\\"EVIDENCE\\\",json.dumps(ev[:1],ensure_ascii=False));print(\\\"THESES\\\",json.dumps(d.get(\\\"new_theses\\\"),ensure_ascii=False)); rr=json.loads(raw); reason=rr[\\\"choices\\\"][0][\\\"message\\\"].get(\\\"reasoning_content\\\",\\\"\\\"); print(\\\"REASON\\\", \\\" \\\".join([x for x in reason.split(\\\"\\\\n\\\") if \\\"financial measure\\\" in x or \\\"70%\\\" in x or \\\"45%\\\" in x])[:3500]); break'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_31","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import sqlite3,json; c=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:poc/data/clean-lean-week-2026-11-14/week-r2/c1/step1/trace.sqlite?mode=ro\\\",uri=True); rows=c.execute(\\\"select sequence,input_id,raw_response,result from trace where result like ?\\\",(\\\"%45%\\\",));\nfor n,i,raw,res in rows:\n d=json.loads(res); ev=[e for e in d.get(\\\"evidence\\\",[]) if \\\"70%\\\" in str(e) and \\\"45%\\\" in str(e)];\n if ev:\n  print(\\\"STEP\\\",n,\\\"ID\\\",i);print(\\\"EVIDENCE\\\",json.dumps(ev[:1],ensure_ascii=False));print(\\\"THESES\\\",json.dumps(d.get(\\\"new_theses\\\"),ensure_ascii=False)); rr=json.loads(raw); reason=rr[\\\"choices\\\"][0][\\\"message\\\"].get(\\\"reasoning_content\\\",\\\"\\\"); print(\\\"REASON\\\", \\\" \\\".join([x for x in reason.split(\\\"\\\\n\\\") if \\\"financial measure\\\" in x or \\\"70%\\\" in x or \\\"45%\\\" in x])[:3500]); break'\"","aggregated_output":"STEP 16 ID gnews:df900d43ab873db9b626aa8e\nEVIDENCE [{\"local_id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"At the time of its second-quarter earnings, NVIDIA projected 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028, well above the 45% analysts had expected at the time, as reported by qz.com.\", \"source_passages\": [\"Nvidia stock climbed roughly 7% after its second-quarter earnings , when revenue more than doubled and the company projected 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028 — well above the 45% analysts had expected at the time.\"], \"original_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"reporting_speaker\": \"qz.com\", \"tier\": \"party_statement\", \"statement_date\": null, \"statement_date_text\": null, \"evidence_period\": \"fiscal 2028\", \"links\": [], \"figures\": [{\"measure\": \"revenue growth\", \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\", \"value\": \"70%\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": \"company projection made with second-quarter earnings; compared with 45% analysts had expected at the time\"}], \"verified_source_passages\": [\"Nvidia stock climbed roughly 7% after its second-quarter earnings , when revenue more than doubled and the company projected 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028 — well above the 45% analysts had expected at the time.\"], \"source_passages_repaired\": [false]}]\nTHESES []\nREASON 2. Revenue and net income each expected to grow by roughly 90% or more in current fiscal year — this is a forecast/estimate (Bloomberg-reported? Actually \"according to Bloomberg\" applies to valuation). \"even as revenue and net income are each expected to grow by roughly 90% or more in its current fiscal year.\" That's a consensus estimate-ish. Is it useful? It's the company's expected revenue growth. Open theses: clean-1 (credit support/guarantees) and clean-2 (purchase/supply commitments). A revenue growth expectation doesn't inform either. Hmm. Is it a condition? It's an estimate without a named party? \"expected to\" — attributed to no one specifically; maybe consensus. Could keep as evidence with empty links? The CONDITION says: a company's own financial figures—reported result, guidance, or a named party's or consensus estimate—lists its useful figures. Its results, guidance and estimates, margins included, inform an open thesis about its revenue or earnings path when they bear on that path. There's no open thesis about revenue/earnings path here. So links stay empty. But should it be returned at all? It is a forecast of revenue growth ~90% for the current fiscal year — an analyst would note it, but it's a vague consensus expectation (no specific figure besides \"roughly 90% or more\"). Hmm, \"roughly 90% or more\" is a figure of sorts. The closing check: \"a general statement that demand stays strong\" isn't kept; this is a growth forecast. Section 2 CONDITION: \"a retailer's quarterly same-store sales, an airline's guidance for next year's unit costs and analysts' consensus earnings for a bank are\" useful. So consensus revenue growth ~90% for the current fiscal year is useful. It can change what an analyst expects of revenue. It's kept with empty links (no open thesis about revenue path). Hmm but wait — \"Do not open a thesis about a financial measure's level, growth, margin or guidance for any period, on its own.\" So no new thesis. So evidence with empty links. That's fine. 3. Gross margin 75% last quarter; analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg show it sliding under 72% by the fourth quarter, with recovery expected thereafter; rising memory chip costs a central factor. This is a margin forecast — useful figure (margin). Does an open thesis cover it? clean-1: credit support/guarantees. clean-2: purchase and supply commitments. Neither covers gross margin. Hmm — clean-2 asks about purchase/supply commitments amounts, an obligation disclosure. Gross margin compression due to memory chip costs informs supply/input costs... but the thesis question is specifically about disclosed purchase commitment amounts in 10-K. The margin forecast does not inform that amount directly. So empty links? Hmm. \"Do not open a thesis about a financial measure's level, growth, margin or guidance for any period, on its own.\" So no new thesis about gross margin. So kept as evidence with empty links. 7. Nvidia stock climbed ~7% after Q2 earnings, when revenue more than doubled and the company projected 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028 — well above 45% analysts had expected at the time. This is important: \"the company projected 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028\" — company guidance. Hmm — it's retold guidance (the Q2 earnings already reported). The closing check: \"Do not drop a reported step, figure, guidance, forecast or decision because the item retells it\" — so keep. But wait: the item describes Q2 earnings and guidance as past (\"stock climbed roughly 7% after its\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_32","type":"agent_message","text":"The stored run confirms a concrete loss: it kept NVIDIA’s 70% growth outlook and the earlier 45% analyst expectation, but opened no question and left that fact unlinked. The rule explains that outcome; the later decision says such figures should inform related questions, so I’m checking what is and is not proven about delivery."}}
